var tafsirData_38 = {
  "1": "The chapter opens with the single Arabic letter Ṣãd, one of the disconnected letters that appear at the beginning of several surahs. The precise meaning of these letters is known fully only to Allah, yet scholars explain that they serve multiple purposes. They immediately capture the listener's attention, especially in an oral culture where eloquence was prized. They also present a profound challenge: this Quran is composed of the very same letters that the Arabs used in their speech, yet they cannot produce anything like it.\n\nThus the disjointed letter stands as a miracle in itself, pointing to the divine origin of the message. Immediately after this letter comes an oath: \"By the Quran, full of reminders.\" Allah swears by the Quran, describing it as being full of reminders.\n\nThe word \"reminders\" translates the Arabic dhikr, which carries the meaning of remembrance, admonition, honour, and renown. This Book is saturated with everything that human beings need to remember their Creator, their purpose, and their final return. It reminds them of the truths written in their own souls and of the lessons from the past nations. The oath elevates the Quran to the status of the highest witness, indicating that the truth being sworn upon is of immense gravity.\n\nThe surah then proceeds to demonstrate how those who turn away from these reminders fall into arrogance and face the consequences. This opening therefore sets the tone: the Quran is a repository of indispensable reminders, and rejecting it is not simply a mistake but a wilful choice born of pride.\n\nThe mention of the letter Ṣãd also connects to the title of the surah, making it recognisable and reinforcing the miraculous nature of the revelation.",
  "2": "The oath by the Quran naturally leads to the assertion that this revealed Book is indeed the truth. The verse states, \"yet the disbelievers are entrenched in arrogance and opposition.\" The two words used for \"arrogance\" and \"opposition\" are ʿizzah and shiqāq in the original. ʿIzzah often means honour or might, but here it describes a sinful pride, an inflated sense of self-importance that prevents a person from submitting to the truth. Instead of honouring the truth, the disbelievers feel that accepting it would lower their status among their people or threaten their leadership. Shiqāq means splitting, hostility, and entrenched opposition. They not only reject the message but actively fight against it, creating a rift between themselves and the Prophet, and between themselves and their own innate knowledge of the truth. Their stance is not a passive disbelief but a deep, active resistance rooted in the ego. The verse strips away any intellectual pretence they might claim; it exposes the real ailment as an internal disease of pride. This diagnosis is timeless. Often people reject clear guidance not because the evidence is lacking but because they do not want to concede that they have been wrong, or they fear losing social standing. The Quran tells the Prophet, and by extension every believer, that the hostility he faces arises from hearts that have chosen arrogance over humility before their Lord.",
  "3": "The disbelievers are now invited to reflect on the ruined remains of earlier civilisations. The command \"Imagine how many peoples We destroyed before them\" points to the well-known stories of ʿĀd, Thamūd, the people of Noah, and others, whose traces the Arabs could still see in their travels.\n\nThe verse then captures a tragic moment: \"and they cried out when it was too late to escape.\" When the punishment arrived, they would scream for help, beg for a second chance, and call upon their Lord—but only after the decree had been sealed. This cry is described as wa-lāta ḥīna manāṣ, meaning there was no time for refuge or escape. The very moment they had been warned about for generations arrived suddenly, and all their previous mockery turned into desperate, futile pleas. The lesson is chilling: repentance is accepted as long as the window of life remains open, but once the final blow comes, that door slams shut. The Quraysh were walking the same path of denial as those earlier communities. By pointing to the past, the Quran connects the present rejection to a recurring historical pattern, warning that divine laws do not show favouritism. A society that collectively defies its messenger and persists in wrongdoing will eventually face the consequences, either in this world or the next.\n\nThis is meant to stir the heart into urgent reflection before it is too late to cry out meaningfully.",
  "4": "Here the focus shifts directly to the Quraysh. They are astonished that a warner—a prophet bringing a dire warning—has emerged from their own people rather than from the ranks of the angels. Their surprise reveals a deep-seated assumption that a messenger should be of a different, superior type of being, forgetting that a human messenger best understands human struggles and can be a relatable model. In their amazement, they scramble for labels to discredit him. They call him a magician and a total liar at the same time. The contradiction is revealing: a magician relies on trickery to deceive people, while a liar fabricates stories. By combining both accusations, they show that they have no coherent criticism; they are simply throwing whatever slander comes to mind.\n\nHistorically, this was the tactic of the Makkan chiefs when the Prophet began calling them publicly. They had known him for decades as Al-Amīn, the Trustworthy, yet overnight they branded him a sorcerer and a fraud. The accusation of magic was particularly common because the Quran’s eloquence moved hearts in a way that their poetry could not, and the only explanation they could muster for its effect was some kind of supernatural spell. The verse exposes the irrational root of their rejection: it is not based on evidence but on shock that one of their own would dare challenge their way of life.",
  "5": "The pagans’ shock deepens as they confront the core of the Prophet’s message: pure monotheism. They ask, \"Has he reduced all the gods to One God?\n\n\" The word \"reduced\" is particularly telling. It implies that in their view, the Prophet was diminishing the divine, shrinking a colourful pantheon into a single, supreme Being. For the Quraysh, their many deities served as intermediaries, sources of pride, and symbols of tribal identity. To worship only Allah meant dismantling the entire religious, social, and economic framework they had inherited from their forefathers. Their exclamation, \"Indeed, this is something totally astonishing,\" reveals that they found the concept of one God without partners utterly strange, even though the traces of Abraham’s monotheism still lingered in Arabia. The verse captures the perennial human resistance to tawḥīd. People often prefer a religious system that allows them to keep their attachments and desires while still performing rituals, rather than a faith that demands exclusive devotion and comprehensive obedience to one God. The astonishment here is ironic, because the most natural truth is that creation has one Creator. That it seemed astonishing to them was a sign of how far they had drifted from the fitrah, the primordial human disposition.",
  "6": "The elite leaders of Quraysh, feeling their authority threatened, did not remain passive. They went forth among the people to rally them against the Prophet’s call. Their instruction to \"carry on\" means to persist in their normal lives and religious practices, refusing to be shaken. They specifically commanded the people to \"stand firm in devotion to your gods,\" openly declaring loyalty to the idols. Then they offered what they thought was a clever interpretation of the Prophet’s motives: \"Certainly this is just a scheme for power.\" They accused him of using religion as a cover to gain leadership and dominance over them. This was a calculated political move, painting the Prophet not as a sincere messenger but as an ambitious man seeking to replace the existing power structure. The charge reveals more about their own mindset than about him. They measured others by their own standards; they valued tribal influence and leadership, so they assumed that anyone calling for change must have the same worldly goals. In reality, the Prophet lived a life of simplicity and faced persecution without any personal gain.\n\nThe verse teaches that truth is often met with accusations of hidden agendas by those who stand to lose their own position.",
  "7": "The chiefs, having accused the Prophet of seeking power, now turn to arguing from tradition. They assert, \"We have never heard of this in the previous faith.\" By \"the previous faith\" they likely meant the religion they had inherited from their ancestors, the remnants of the Abrahamic legacy that had become thoroughly corrupted with idol worship. They claimed that the message of pure monotheism and accountability after death was an innovation unheard of in their community’s religious memory. Their appeal was to the religion of their fathers, as if mere antiquity guaranteed truth. Then they delivered their verdict: \"This is nothing but a fabrication,\" an invented lie. This argument from tradition is one of the most common shields used against prophets throughout history. People feel a deep comfort in the familiar, and any call to return to the original purity of faith can appear as a strange, unwelcome disruption. The Quran later demolishes this excuse by showing that the true faith of Abraham was exactly what the Prophet was reviving. The disbelievers had forgotten their own roots, yet they clung to the distorted version that justified their idolatry.",
  "8": "The elite express their wounded pride more openly. They ask, \"Has the Reminder been revealed only to him out of all of us?\" They could not fathom that Allah would choose an orphan, not one of the wealthy chiefs from the two great cities Makkah and Ṭāʾif, to receive revelation. In their worldview, status and wealth were signs of divine favour, so a true prophet should have come from the upper class.\n\nThe verse then gives Allah’s penetrating diagnosis of their real condition: they are not engaged in a sincere inquiry; rather they are in doubt of the Reminder itself. Their question is not a genuine search for truth but a mask for deep-seated scepticism and resentment.\n\nThe second part of the verse adds a stern reality: they have not yet tasted His punishment. Their present arrogance and casual dismissal of the message stem from a sense of security. Once the punishment they mock descends, all doubts will vanish, but that will be too late for the faith that benefits. It is a reminder that the delay of punishment is a test and a mercy, not a sign that the warnings are false.",
  "9": "This verse presents a rhetorical question that cuts away any legitimacy from their objections. \"Or is it because they possess the treasuries of the mercy of your Lord?\" Prophethood and revelation are among the greatest manifestations of Allah’s mercy. If the disbelievers believe they have the right to decide who receives this mercy, they are effectively claiming to control the treasuries of the Lord.\n\nThe question is obviously absurd. No human being owns the sources of divine mercy; Allah alone distributes His favour according to His infinite wisdom. He is described as \"the Almighty,\" whose will cannot be resisted, and \"the Giver of all bounties,\" who bestows generously without being subject to human criteria. If they did own these treasuries, they would hoard them out of stinginess, but Allah’s generosity is boundless. The verse subtly reminds the Prophet that the rejection he faces is not a sign of his lack of worth; it is a sign that his opponents fail to recognise that the distribution of spiritual gifts is entirely in the hands of the Almighty. This reorients the heart of every believer to accept that Allah grants understanding, prophethood, and wisdom to whom He wills.",
  "10": "The series of sarcastic challenges continues. Do they possess the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything between them? If they did, they might have some authority to dictate how the universe is run and to whom revelation is sent. But they own nothing; they are themselves small creatures living on a tiny part of the earth.\n\nThe next line delivers a powerful, ironic dare: \"Let them then climb their way to heaven, if their claim is true.\" The phrase \"their way\" refers to the means of ascent, the ropes or pathways that might allow one to reach the heavens.\n\nThis is a direct challenge to their pretensions of power. If they truly think they have a say in celestial affairs, let them physically ascend to the heavens and manage the distribution of mercy themselves. The impossibility of this task exposes their utter powerlessness.\n\nHistorically, some Arab pagans did have myths about ascending to heaven, but this verse turns their own concepts against them to highlight their helplessness. The one who controls the heavens is Allah, and He sends down revelation from there as He pleases.",
  "11": "After dismantling their arguments, the Quran describes the disbelieving faction in the bluntest terms. \"This is just another enemy force bound for defeat.\" The word \"force\" translates jund, a military term referring to an assembled army. The Quraysh, who saw themselves as a mighty, unconquerable community, are told they are nothing more than one more hostile army among the many that have opposed the truth throughout history.\n\nThe phrase \"bound for defeat\" is a decisive prophecy. The Arabic mā hunālika mahzūm literally means \"defeated right there.\" It points to a specific place and time where their collective power will be shattered. Many commentators identify this with the defeat of the Quraysh at the Battle of Badr, where their leading chiefs were killed and their pride crushed. The verse gave the early Muslims immense psychological strength. They were outnumbered and persecuted, yet Allah assured them that their oppressors were part of a defeated force by divine decree. This teaches that the apparent strength of the enemies of truth is an illusion destined to collapse.",
  "12": "The Quran now supports its warning by listing famous destroyed nations. The people of Noah denied their messenger for centuries, and their final end was the great flood that wiped out every disbeliever. Then came ʿĀd, the powerful nation of the prophet Hūd, who were blessed with tall stature and impressive buildings but refused to submit to Allah. They were annihilated by a screaming wind.\n\nThe third mentioned is \"Pharaoh of the mighty structures.\" The phrase \"mighty structures\" renders dhī al-awtād, which literally means \"possessor of stakes.\" Scholars explain this in several complementary ways. It can refer to the pyramids and great buildings of Egypt, which stand like pegs driven into the ground. It can also refer to the massive army encampments that required thousands of tent pegs, indicating military might. Some interpretations mention the cruel stakes upon which Pharaoh tortured his victims. In any case, the imagery conveys immense, tyrannical power.\n\nYet all that power could not save Pharaoh from drowning in the sea. Listing these nations in quick succession shows that no matter how advanced or mighty a civilisation appears, if it stands against the truth, its end is certain.",
  "13": "The catalogue of destruction continues. Thamūd were the people of Ṣāliḥ, known for carving magnificent homes out of the mountains. When they arrogantly killed the miraculous she-camel, an earthquake or a thunderous blast wiped them out. Then the people of Lot are mentioned. Their sin was not just idolatry but also their infamous immoral practices that went against human nature. They were punished with a rain of stones and the turning of their land upside down.\n\nFinally, \"the residents of the Forest\" refers to the people of Madyan, the nation of Shuʿayb. They lived in a lush area of thick trees but were destroyed by a dreadful earthquake and a cloud of fire because of their persistent cheating in trade and rejection of their prophet.\n\nThe verse ends by summarising them all: \"These were all enemy forces.\" They formed a united front of enmity against their respective messengers, and they all met the same catastrophic end. This serves as a powerful warning to the Makkans that they are not a special case; they belong to the same line of hostile coalitions, and unless they change, their fate will mirror that of those who came before.",
  "14": "The verse brings the series to a concise and definitive conclusion. The Arabic in kullun illā kadhdhaba ar-rusula means every single one of those mentioned nations, without exception, rejected the messenger sent to them. This was not a partial rejection or a mere doubt; it was a decisive, obstinate denial. Because of this universal rejection, the outcome was inevitable: \"so My punishment was justified. \" The word \"justified\" translates ḥaqqa, meaning it came true, it became a reality, it was deserved and decreed. The punishment was not an arbitrary act of anger; it was the just recompense for persistent, wilful denial after clear proofs had been presented. The verse ties together the stories of the previous verses into a single principle: when a messenger delivers the message with clarity and the people respond with nothing but rejection and mockery, divine justice requires that they be held accountable.\n\nThis principle applies across time. The Quraysh, who were now mocking the Prophet and asking for the punishment to be hastened, were positioning themselves under the same law. The verse invites them to learn from history before history repeats itself with them as the subject.",
  "15": "The focus returns to the present disbelievers. After hearing about the diverse and prolonged punishments that afflicted earlier peoples, they might think that their own punishment is far off or that they are safe. This verse corrects that assumption. \"These pagans are awaiting nothing but a single Blast.\n\n\" The word \"Blast\" translates ṣayḥah, which means a tremendous shout, cry, or thunderous sound. It can refer to the first blow of the Trumpet on the Day of Resurrection, or to a sudden destructive cry that seizes a people in this world, as happened to Thamūd. The key point is that it is a singular, instantaneous event. Then the verse adds the chilling description: \"that cannot be stopped.\" The original phrase mā lahā min fawāq holds layers of meaning. Fawāq can mean an interval, a break, or a return. So this Blast has no pause, no gap, no respite, and no possibility of being reversed or held back once it comes. There will be no time for repentance, no opportunity to negotiate, and no second chance. The entire complex order of their lives will be shattered in a moment. This stark warning is meant to shake the complacent heart into realising the urgency of faith.",
  "16": "Far from taking the warning seriously, the disbelievers meet it with sarcasm. They address Allah directly, saying, \"Our Lord! Hasten for us our share of the punishment before the Day of Reckoning.\" This was an act of extreme defiance. When the Prophet warned them of a painful punishment in the Hereafter, they would mockingly ask him to bring it forward, as if to prove that his threats were empty. Their demand for a \"share\" implies they saw the punishment as something they could receive in this world, almost as if they considered it a trivial matter that they could settle early.\n\nThe phrase \"before the Day of Reckoning\" shows they were still thinking in terms of a this-worldly settling of accounts, not the eternal consequences. This mockery is recorded in several surahs. It reflects a deep level of insolence, where a person becomes so hardened that divine warnings only increase them in ridicule. The Prophet is being told of this so that he knows such reactions are not new and not a sign of his failure. It also serves as a lesson that mocking sacred truths is a spiritual disease that pushes a person closer to the very punishment they joke about, without them realising what they are inviting upon themselves.",
  "17": "In the face of such mockery, the divine instruction comes: \"Be patient, O Prophet, with what they say.\" Patience is the protective shield of the prophet. But the command is not an isolated, dry instruction; it is immediately followed by a source of comfort and strength: the remembrance of righteous servants of the past. \"And remember Our servant, David.\n\n\" The phrase \"Our servant\" is a title of immense honour, showing David’s total devotion to Allah. He is described as \"the man of strength,\" which encompasses physical strength, mighty kingship, and profound spiritual strength in worship and in battle for the cause of Allah. The most beautiful quality is mentioned next: \"Indeed, he constantly turned to Allah.\n\n\" The Arabic word is awwāb, an intensive form meaning one who repeatedly and deeply turns back to God in repentance, in remembrance, and in obedience. David was not a flawless angel; he was a human being who turned to his Lord with a heart full of humility and devotion. By connecting the command to be patient with the story of David, the Quran teaches the Prophet that patience is cultivated by remembering the models of godly strength and by continually turning one’s heart to Allah. David’s life becomes a mirror in which the believer can find encouragement.",
  "18": "The verse begins to unveil the special favours that Allah bestowed upon David. The mountains, despite their massive and seemingly unmovable nature, were subjected to join him in hymn. When David would recite the Psalms—the Zabūr—with his exceptionally beautiful voice, the mountains would echo his glorification of Allah. They did not merely resonate with sound; they engaged in actual tasbīḥ, praising the Creator. The times mentioned, \"in the evening and after sunrise,\" indicate that this phenomenon accompanied his daily routine of worship. The evening (ʿashīy) covers the later part of the day leading to sunset, and the time \"after sunrise\" (ishrāq) refers to the early morning when the sun has risen and its light spreads over the mountains. This pairing of times highlights that David’s remembrance of Allah bookended the day.\n\nThe image is profoundly moving: a prophet-king standing amidst nature, his voice lifting up praises, and the towering mountains around him joining in a cosmic symphony of worship. It serves as a reminder that the entire creation is in a state of devotion to Allah, and when a human being’s heart becomes fully aligned with that devotion, even the inanimate world around him becomes a partner in glorifying God.",
  "19": "The chain of subjection continues with the birds. They are described as being \"flocking together\" with him.\n\nThe Arabic maḥshūrah means gathered and assembled, as if the birds were a disciplined army responding to his call. Birds, by their nature, are scattered in the sky, but for David they would come together and fly around him, joining his chants of praise.\n\nThe phrase \"all turned to him\" is profoundly significant. The original says kullun lahu awwāb. Every mountain and every bird was awwāb to him—not to David as an object of worship, but they all repeatedly turned with him in obedience to Allah’s command, responding to his lead in worship. This illustrates the idea that when a servant of Allah reaches a high station of closeness to his Lord, the entire environment is affected by his spiritual state. The scene is one of perfect harmony: a human being, the mountains, and the birds united in a single act of remembering the Creator. It teaches that true leadership in Allah’s sight brings everything into a state of worshipful submission, not through oppression but through the light of devotion that emanates from a pure heart.",
  "20": "The final verse in this passage summarises the worldly and spiritual gifts granted to David. \"We strengthened his kingship\" means that Allah made his rule firm, stable, and respected. His kingdom was not merely a political structure; it was a divinely supported dominion where justice prevailed. Enemies were subdued, and the land was secure under his authority. Alongside this worldly strength, he was given \"wisdom. \" Wisdom, or ḥikmah, in the Quranic context includes prophethood, deep understanding of the divine law, the ability to discern right from wrong, and the capacity to apply knowledge in the best possible way.\n\nFinally, the verse mentions \"sound judgment,\" which translates faṣl al-khiṭāb. Literally it means \"the decisive speech,\" but it refers to the ability to deliver a clear, conclusive judgment in disputes. Some scholars specify that it refers to the principle that the burden of proof lies on the claimant and the oath is upon the defendant—a foundational rule in Islamic jurisprudence. Others see it as eloquence and clarity in delivering the truth. Taken together, this verse shows that David was a complete leader: his authority was solid, his inner knowledge was profound, and his ability to resolve conflicts among people was exceptional.\n\nThe next passage will test this very gift of judgment with the story of the two disputants.",
  "21": "After showing how David was granted wisdom and sound judgment, the Quran now presents a powerful story that illustrates the testing of that very gift. The verse asks, \"Has the story of the two plaintiffs reached you?\" This rhetorical question draws the Prophet’s attention and by extension ours to a narrative packed with lessons. The two plaintiffs are described as having \"scaled the wall of David’s sanctuary. \" The word for sanctuary is miḥrāb, which in David’s context refers to his private chamber of worship, a fortified place where he would seclude himself for devotion. The fact that they climbed over the wall rather than entering through the proper door signals that something unusual and divinely orchestrated was taking place. They were not ordinary litigants; many scholars identify them as angels sent in human form to test David. Their sudden, startling entrance through an unexpected route was the first indication to David that this case carried a deeper purpose. The verse sets the stage for a divine trial, a test not of David’s legal knowledge in a simple sense but of his inner impartiality, his humility, and his swiftness in turning back to Allah when he realised the lesson.\n\nThe mention of the story being directed to the Prophet also consoles him, showing that previous prophets underwent trials of judgment and character, yet they emerged beloved to Allah through sincere repentance.",
  "22": "The verse describes David’s immediate reaction: he was startled, even frightened, by their sudden appearance in his private chamber. His alarm is natural—they had bypassed all guards and barriers, and their manner was intense. They immediately sought to calm him, saying, \"Have no fear.\" Then they explained themselves: \"We are merely two in a dispute. \" This reassurance lowered the tension while simultaneously framing what was to follow as a judicial matter. Their request is detailed and instructive: \"So judge between us with truth—do not go beyond it—and guide us to the right way.\" They are asking for a verdict based solely on truth, without any deviation or injustice.\n\nThe phrase \"do not go beyond it\" means do not exceed the bounds of fairness even slightly. And \"guide us to the right way\" means lead us to what is just and upright. This elaborate plea placed David in the full responsibility of his role as a judge. It also subtly foreshadowed the lesson he was about to learn. Even a prophet-judge must remain vigilant against any factor that could cloud pure justice, including the natural human tendency to sympathise with one party before hearing both sides fully. The entire exchange is set up so that David would give a ruling that, while legally correct on the surface, would carry a deeper personal lesson for him.",
  "23": "One of the two plaintiffs steps forward to present his case. He points to his companion and says, \"This is my brother.\n\n\" The word \"brother\" here means a partner or fellow in faith or community, not necessarily a blood sibling, though it evokes the closeness that should preclude oppression. He then lays out the stark inequality: \"He has ninety-nine sheep while I have only one. \" The imagery is simple and powerful. The one with overwhelming resources is not satisfied; he demands the single sheep owned by the poorer brother.\n\nThe phrase \"Still he asked me to give it up to him\" reveals the greed and lack of contentment. The richer brother’s demand is backed not by need or right but by sheer force of rhetoric: \"overwhelming me with his argument. \" He used his powerful speech to overpower the weaker party, leaving him with no effective reply. The case appears straightforward: a clear act of injustice by a strong partner against a vulnerable one. David’s instinct as a just ruler would immediately be stirred.\n\nYet the entire allegory is laden with spiritual meaning. David himself had many wives, and his test would involve him being reminded that even a powerful person can fall into the trap of desiring what belongs to another, or of acting without full impartiality. The story is building to that profound moment of self-realisation.",
  "24": "David delivered his verdict quickly, based on the apparent facts: \"He has definitely wronged you in demanding to add your sheep to his.\" The ruling was correct given the undisputed account. He then added a general, wise observation: \"And certainly many partners wrong each other, except those who believe and do good—but how few are they!\n\n\" This statement acknowledged that in business and personal partnerships, injustice is common; only the righteous who are guided by faith treat their partners justly, and they are lamentably few. The moment he pronounced this judgment, the deeper reality struck him. The verse says, \"Then David realized that We had tested him.\" He understood that the two plaintiffs were angels sent by Allah, and that the parable of the ninety-nine sheep and the one sheep was a mirror held up to his own situation. He had been tested in his capacity as a ruler and as a man, and he recognised that he had been swift to judge without perhaps seeking the full picture, or that a subtle desire had crept in. His response was immediate and profound: he asked for his Lord’s forgiveness, fell down in prostration, and turned to Him in repentance. The prostration here is so significant that it became a sunnah, a prostration of recitation for anyone who reads this verse. David’s total humility before Allah is the model for every believer: the recognition of one’s human limitations and the urgent, wholehearted return to Allah.",
  "25": "The verse brings relief and elevation. Allah says, \"So We forgave that for him.\" The \"that\" refers to whatever lapse or hastiness in judgment occurred during the incident. The swift and complete forgiveness demonstrates Allah’s mercy towards His beloved servant who repented sincerely.\n\nThe verse then immediately transitions to reward: \"And he will indeed have a status of closeness to Us and an honourable destination.\" The phrase \"closeness to Us\" translates zulfā, an intimate nearness that is a spiritual station of the highest order. Despite the test and the human moment, David’s rank was not diminished; through his repentance, he was raised to greater nearness to his Lord. And the \"honourable destination\" refers to the beautiful return in the Hereafter, the gardens of bliss and the highest stations of Paradise. This verse is crucial for understanding how Allah views His prophets. Their small lapses, when followed by immediate and deep repentance, become means for drawing even closer to Allah. It also teaches ordinary believers that sin is not the end of one’s spiritual journey if it is followed by genuine tawbah. The shame of the mistake is washed away by the mercy of the Most Forgiving, and the servant’s ultimate standing is determined by that return to Allah, not by the initial stumble.",
  "26": "After the personal trial and forgiveness, Allah addresses David directly with a set of instructions that form a charter for just leadership. \"O David! We have surely made you an authority in the land.\n\n\" The term khalīfah (authority, vicegerent) is used, indicating that David was placed as a representative of Allah’s rule on earth. With this appointment comes the paramount duty: \"so judge between people with truth. \" This is the core function of his authority, to uphold justice based on divine revelation, not on personal whim. The warning that follows is severe: \"And do not follow your desires or they will lead you astray from Allah’s Way.\" Desires, if not checked, distort judgment, create bias, and pull the heart away from the straight path. The lesson from the sheep parable now becomes explicit. The ultimate consequence of being led astray by desire is described with sobering clarity: \"Surely those who go astray from Allah’s Way will suffer a severe punishment for neglecting the Day of Reckoning.\n\n\" The phrase \"neglecting the Day of Reckoning\" reveals the root cause: when a person forgets that they will stand before Allah and answer for every judgment, they become careless with truth and justice. This verse is not only for rulers but for anyone in a position of authority, including parents, teachers, and anyone who must adjudicate between people.",
  "27": "After the story of David and the principles of justice, the Quran returns to confronting the disbelievers’ worldview. It firmly declares, \"We have not created the heavens and earth and everything in between without purpose.\n\n\" The Arabic bāṭil means falsehood, frivolity, or without any serious objective. This negates the atheistic and materialistic view that the universe is a meaningless, random accident.\n\nThe verse then specifically pinpoints the disbelievers’ assumption: \"as the disbelievers think.\" Their behaviour—denying resurrection, rejecting accountability, living for worldly gain—stems from this false belief that creation has no ultimate purpose. Because they think there is no judgment, they act as if their desires are the only law.\n\nThe verse then delivers a stern warning: \"So woe to the disbelievers because of the Fire! \" The word \"woe\" translates wayl, an expression of severe destruction and grief. The Fire that awaits them is the logical consequence of their wilful denial of the purpose of existence. The connection to David’s instruction is subtle but deep: David was warned to judge with truth and not follow desire because he would face the Day of Reckoning. The disbelievers deny that very Day, and therefore their judgment and their entire life purpose become corrupted, leading to their ruin.",
  "28": "This verse poses two powerful rhetorical questions that appeal directly to human reason and an innate sense of justice. The creation of the heavens and earth with purpose logically demands that the Creator differentiate between those who fulfill that purpose and those who corrupt it.\n\nThe first question asks, \"Or should We treat those who believe and do good like those who make mischief throughout the land?\" The believers who couple faith with righteous deeds are the ones who align themselves with the purpose of creation. The mufsidūn, the mischief-makers, are those who spread corruption, oppress others, and cause ruin on earth. To treat both groups identically would be a manifest injustice, and Allah is the Most Just.\n\nThe second question reinforces this: \"Or should We treat the righteous like the wicked?\" The righteous are the mutaqūn, those who are constantly conscious of Allah and guard against evil. The wicked are the fujjār, those who burst through moral boundaries without restraint. The structure of the verse implies that it is impossible for divine justice to equate these opposites.\n\nTherefore, there must be a Day of Reckoning where each group receives its due recompense. This verse is a rational proof for the Afterlife. Without it, the moral order of the universe would be incomplete, and the suffering of the innocent and the tyranny of the wicked would remain ultimately unaddressed.",
  "29": "Having established that the universe is purposeful and that justice requires a final judgment, Allah now explains how this guidance has been given. The Quran is described as \"a blessed Book.\n\n\" The word mubārak means full of enduring, ever-increasing goodness, spiritual benefit, and divine grace. It is a Book that transforms lives, heals hearts, and provides the criteria for justice that David was commanded to uphold. The primary purpose of its revelation is stated clearly: \"so that they may contemplate its verses.\" Contemplation, or tadabbur, is not a quick, superficial reading. It means to deeply reflect upon the meanings of the verses, to consider their implications, and to allow them to penetrate the heart.\n\nThe verse then specifies the intended audience of this contemplation: \"and people of reason may be mindful. \" The ulū al-albāb are those who possess sound, penetrating intellects. They are the ones who do not let desires cloud their thinking. For them, the Quran is the ultimate source of remembrance and guidance. This verse directly refutes the notion that the Quran is to be recited without understanding. It mandates a thoughtful engagement with the text, connecting reason and revelation. The disbelievers’ problem is not a lack of intelligence but a failure to use their intellects to contemplate the Book that has been sent to them.",
  "30": "The narrative now shifts seamlessly from David to his son and successor, Solomon. \"And We blessed David with Solomon\" indicates that Solomon was a tremendous gift from Allah to David, a son who would inherit not just the kingdom but also prophethood and wisdom. The verse then praises Solomon in the highest terms: \"what an excellent servant he was!\n\n\" The Arabic niʿma al-ʿabd is a divine commendation that carries immense weight. Solomon was a king of unparalleled power and wealth, yet his defining characteristic was his excellence as a servant of Allah. Then the verse adds the very same quality highlighted in David: \"Indeed, he constantly turned to Allah.\n\n\" The word awwāb, the one repeatedly turning back to God in devotion and repentance, links father and son in the deepest spiritual bond. Despite Solomon’s vast dominion over humans, jinn, animals, and even the wind, his heart remained attached only to his Lord. This introduction sets up the stories of Solomon’s trials and blessings that follow. It also reassures the Prophet Muhammad that the legacy of prophethood is a chain of devoted servants, each tested and each honoured, and that true greatness lies in the quality of servanthood, not in worldly possessions.",
  "31": "The Quran now transports us to a specific event in Solomon’s life. The command \"Remember\" calls the Prophet and the believers to reflect on this moment. \"When the well-trained, swift horses were paraded before him in the evening.\n\n\" The Arabic describes these horses as aṣ-ṣāfināt al-jiyād. Ṣāfināt refers to horses that stand poised on three legs, touching the ground lightly with the tip of the fourth hoof, a stance of disciplined, noble steeds ready for action. Al-Jiyād means swift, excellent racing horses of the finest breed. These were horses prepared for jihad, for striving in the path of Allah. The time mentioned, \"in the evening,\" is al-ʿashīy, the latter part of the day, a time of beauty and reflection. Solomon, as a king and prophet, was reviewing these magnificent creatures. The parade was not a frivolous display; it was a review of strength prepared for the cause of truth.\n\nHowever, even lawful love and admiration for such blessings can become a test. The detail that this occurred in the evening, which then transitioned towards night, becomes significant for the incident that follows, where his immersion in observing these horses may have distracted him from a particular act of remembrance at its appointed time. The stage is set for a lesson in prioritising the love of Allah above all attachments.",
  "32": "Solomon’s reaction upon seeing the magnificent horses is recorded in his own words: \"I am truly in love with these fine things out of remembrance for Allah.\" The phrase \"I am in love with\" translates aḥbabtu ḥubba, expressing a deep affection. But he immediately qualifies that this love is \"out of remembrance for Allah,\" or more precisely, \"because of the remembrance of my Lord.\" This can be understood in two interconnected ways. One is that his love for these horses stemmed from his awareness that they were a blessing from Allah and a means to carry out His commands, especially jihad.\n\nThus his love was not independent of Allah but rooted in the remembrance of Him. The other understanding is that as he was admiring them, he was continuously remembering Allah in his heart, so his delight was permeated with the consciousness of the Giver.\n\nThe verse then notes, \"until they went out of sight,\" meaning he continued watching them as they raced or were led away, disappearing behind the horizon. He spent so much time in this state of delighted observation that the sun set. Some reports indicate that this caused him to miss the time of the afternoon prayer, ʿAṣr, though he did not wilfully neglect it. This becomes the subtle test: even a permissible, God-conscious love, when it causes a delay in a specific obligatory devotion, requires a corrective response from the servant.",
  "33": "When the horses had vanished from sight, Solomon realised the passage of time and the distraction they had caused. He immediately gave the command, \"Bring them back to me!\" They were returned to him. Then he began to \"rub down their legs and necks. \" The Arabic word translated as \"rub down\" is masḥan, which can mean to pass his hand over them or to strike them with his sword. The classical commentators offer two distinct interpretations, both deeply instructive.\n\nThe first is that he stroked their legs and necks out of affection, honouring them for their role in Allah’s cause, and perhaps dedicating them to the service of God by marking them as waqf (endowed) for jihad. The second interpretation is that he used his sword to cut their legs and necks, sacrificing them. Why would a prophet of God do such a thing? Because these horses, which he loved for the sake of Allah, had inadvertently become a veil that distracted him from the remembrance of Allah at its appointed time. By sacrificing them, he demonstrated in the most dramatic way that nothing—no worldly possession, however valuable and however legitimately loved—could be allowed to compete with his devotion to his Lord. Both interpretations highlight his immediate, uncompromising return to Allah. The action, whatever it was, represented his awwāb nature: a swift turning back, removing the attachment that had momentarily occupied his heart over God’s remembrance.",
  "34": "The Quran now references another trial in Solomon’s life, one that is mentioned briefly and enigmatically. \"And indeed, We tested Solomon, placing a deformed body on his throne.\" The phrase \"deformed body\" translates jasad. There is a well-known tradition, transmitted from some of the early scholars, about the cause of this test. It is reported that Solomon once swore that all his wives would conceive and bear him a son who would fight in the path of Allah, but he did not say \"if Allah wills. \" Out of all his wives, only one conceived and she gave birth to a deformed, lifeless body that was placed on his throne. This was a lesson to him about the absolute dependence of all outcomes on the will of Allah, and the importance of always coupling one’s intention with the recognition of divine decree. Other interpretations exist, but the core lesson remains the same: even the mightiest king is completely powerless without Allah’s will.\n\nThe verse continues, \"then he turned to Allah in repentance. \" Solomon immediately recognised the trial for what it was and returned to his Lord in humility. This swift repentance, mirroring his father David’s response, is the mark of the true servant. The test did not break him; it purified him.\n\nThe verse teaches that trials for the prophets and the righteous are often precisely in those areas where they are strongest, to remind them that all strength is from Allah alone.",
  "35": "In the wake of the trial, Solomon turns to Allah with a deeply personal and profound supplication. \"My Lord! Forgive me.\" He begins by seeking forgiveness, acknowledging any shortcoming that may have necessitated the test. Then he makes a unique request: \"and grant me an authority that will never be matched by anyone after me. \" The word for authority is mulk, meaning a kingdom or dominion. Solomon asked for an exceptional, unprecedented kingdom that would be a sign of his prophethood and a means to manifest Allah’s glory. Importantly, he did not ask for this out of greed or a desire for personal aggrandizement. He asked for it as a divine gift that would remain unmatched, so that its miraculous nature would be clear to all, and so that no one after him would be able to claim an equal status. His request was specifically for something that would not be given to anyone else, which shows that it was for a higher purpose of demonstrating Allah’s power. He ended the supplication with the beautiful words, \"You are indeed the Giver of all bounties.\" He acknowledged that the ultimate source of all gifts is Allah alone, the Wahhāb, the One who gives freely and abundantly without measure. Allah accepted this prayer, and what follows in the next verses is a glimpse of that extraordinary authority.",
  "36": "The answer to Solomon’s prayer begins to unfold. The first gift mentioned is the subjection of the wind. \"So We subjected to him the wind.\" This was not an ordinary breeze but a wind under his direct command. It blew \"gently,\" meaning softly and obediently, according to his will.\n\nThe Arabic rukhāʾan indicates a wind that is easy, pleasant, and not stormy or destructive. It would carry his massive flying carpet or vessel, along with his entourage, to any destination he desired.\n\nThe phrase \"at his command to wherever he pleased\" highlights the precision and completeness of his control. The wind would take him exactly where he wanted, without deviation. Imagine the scene: a prophet-king travelling through the skies with the wind as his subservient mount. This was a manifest miracle that surpassed all ordinary modes of travel. It demonstrated that the One who sent the wind is the One who can make it a servant to His chosen servant. This gift also had a practical purpose: it allowed Solomon to travel great distances to spread the message, to oversee his vast kingdom, and to witness the marvels of Allah’s creation without the hardships of ordinary travel. It was a mercy and a means to facilitate his duties.",
  "37": "The subjection of the jinn was another extraordinary aspect of Solomon’s dominion. The verse specifies two categories of jinn who served him: builders and divers. The builders constructed whatever he required—temples, palaces, and great structures like the Temple in Jerusalem. Their architectural skills were beyond human capacity, enabling the construction of monumental works that stood as signs of the power Allah had granted His prophet. The divers would plunge into the depths of the seas to retrieve pearls, corals, and other treasures. This pairing is striking: the builders conquered the heights of the earth with their structures, and the divers conquered the depths of the oceans. Together, they represented a comprehensive mastery over the physical world.\n\nYet all of this was not through Solomon’s own power; it was Allah who made the jinn subservient to him. The Quran elsewhere mentions that the jinn worked for him as he wished, and that if any of them deviated, they would be punished with a blazing fire. This demonstrates that even the rebellious jinn could be controlled by a prophet who ruled by Allah’s command. The lesson is that true authority is a gift from Allah, not a product of human effort alone. Solomon’s kingdom was a sign pointing to the limitless power of the Creator.",
  "38": "The verse continues with another category of subjection: \"and others bound together in chains.\" This refers to the most rebellious and dangerous devils among the jinn. They were not merely subjugated to work; they were physically restrained in chains and shackles to prevent them from spreading their mischief and evil among people.\n\nThis detail reveals that Solomon’s authority extended even to incarcerating malevolent forces that could otherwise wreak havoc. The chains were a manifestation of divine justice executed through His prophet. It was a purification of the land from satanic influence during his reign. For the ordinary believer, this serves as a powerful reminder that Allah can restrain the forces of evil as He wills, and that in the end, all rebellious powers are under His absolute control. The chains also symbolise the binding of Satan and his hosts that will occur in the Hereafter, giving a foretaste of that ultimate victory of good over evil. Solomon’s earthly kingdom thus becomes a miniature model of the complete divine order that will be established on the Day of Judgment.",
  "39": "After enumerating these immense blessings, Allah speaks directly to Solomon, granting him something even more precious: complete freedom and divine trust. \"This is Our gift.\" The use of the word \"gift\" (ʿaṭāʾ) indicates that all of this was a pure, unearned bestowal from Allah’s generosity. The instruction that follows is astonishing in its scope: \"so give or withhold as you wish, never to be called to account.\" Solomon was given full discretion over this vast kingdom. He could bestow from its riches upon whomever he pleased, or he could withhold. He was not required to render a strict accounting to Allah for how he distributed this specific gift, because Allah knew that Solomon’s heart was so purified and his wisdom so complete that whatever he decided would be just and in accordance with divine pleasure.\n\nThis is a unique honour, a testament to Solomon’s elevated station. It also carries a profound lesson: when a servant reaches the highest level of servanthood, where his will is completely aligned with Allah’s will, he is granted a kind of spiritual liberty. His choices naturally manifest what is pleasing to Allah, so no external accountability is needed for those matters.\n\nThis is not a licence for laxity but a recognition of spiritual perfection.",
  "40": "The section on Solomon concludes with the very same beautiful promise that was given to his father David. \"And he will indeed have a status of closeness to Us and an honourable destination.\" The word zulfā (closeness) reappears, establishing that despite his unprecedented worldly kingdom, Solomon’s ultimate reward is nearness to Allah.\n\nThis is the true measure of success, not the wind, the jinn, or the treasures. The \"honourable destination\" is the bliss of Paradise, a place of beauty and honour that far surpasses any earthly dominion. The verse ties father and son together in their final station, showing that both, after their respective tests and their sincere turning to Allah, attained the highest ranks. It serves as a deeply comforting close to the stories of David and Solomon for the Prophet Muhammad, and for every believer. No matter how great the trial or how magnificent the blessing, the ultimate goal is the same: to be brought near to Allah and to enter the honourable abode.\n\nThis promise then stands in stark contrast to the fate of the disbelievers described earlier, who have no such destination awaiting them.",
  "41": "The Quran now moves from the majesty of Solomon’s kingdom to the profound trial of Job, a shift that carries a deep message. The command “And remember Our servant Job” uses the same honoured title, “Our servant,” linking Job to David and Solomon in the chain of beloved worshippers. Job is presented as a model of patience. He cried out to his Lord, but note the nature of his complaint: “Satan has afflicted me with distress and suffering. ” He did not curse his fate or question Allah’s wisdom; he directed his grievance towards Satan, recognizing that the affliction, though decreed by Allah as a test, was unleashed through the enemy. The words “distress” (nusb) and “suffering” (ʿadhāb) together paint a picture of comprehensive hardship—exhaustion of body and soul, loss of wealth, the death of his children, and a prolonged, repulsive disease that made people shun him.\n\nYet Job’s patience was legendary. Historical traditions tell us that he endured for years without a single word of discontent, and when his wife, in a moment of weakness, suggested he pray for relief, he reminded her that they had enjoyed blessings for far longer and that it was only right to bear trials with gratitude. His cry here is not an objection but a humble, suppressed plea, directed to the only One who could lift the harm. Satan’s role is clearly stated: he cannot afflict without Allah’s permission, and his goal is to drive the servant to despair. Job’s prayer is a masterclass in how to complain to Allah: acknowledge the enemy’s role, but place all hope solely in the Lord’s mercy. This teaches that even prophets are tested, and the greatest test is reserved for the most beloved, to elevate their rank.\n\nThe verse also subtly refutes the idea that material loss or sickness is a sign of divine displeasure; rather, it can be the very means of drawing close to Allah.",
  "42": "The divine response to Job’s patient cry was immediate and miraculous. Allah commanded him: “Stomp your foot.” The earth, which had become a place of isolation and sickness for him, would now become a source of healing. As he struck the ground, a spring burst forth. This was not just any water; it was “a cool and refreshing spring.\n\n” The Arabic bārid (cool) and sharāb (drinking) combine two essential qualities: the water was perfectly cool to soothe his fevered skin, and it was pure and sweet for drinking, restoring his dehydrated body. The command to use it “for washing and drinking” indicates a complete cure: external washing to cleanse the disease that covered his body, and internal drinking to rejuvenate his organs. This moment captures the transformative power of divine mercy. After years of affliction, the remedy was instant, effortless, and located exactly beneath his feet, showing that relief had always been closer than he imagined, withheld only for a wisdom-filled period. The instruction to stomp also implies an active role; Job had to make a movement, however small, to receive the cure, teaching that while healing is from Allah, the servant must engage with the means provided. The coolness of the water is especially significant, as many scholars note that Job’s affliction involved severe, burning inflammation. The cure through water also prefigures the rivers of Paradise that will wash away all pain. The entire scene is one of profound divine tenderness: the patient servant was not forgotten, and at the appointed moment, the relief came in a way that was both physically restorative and spiritually uplifting, a tangible sign of Allah’s care.",
  "43": "The restoration did not stop at physical healing; it extended to all that Job had lost. “And We gave him back his family, twice as many.” Job had lost his children during the trial, and now Allah blessed him with even more, either by reviving those who had died or by granting him new offspring. Some reports say that the original children were brought back to life and that additional children were born, so that his family literally doubled. This doubling is a pattern of divine generosity: Job’s patience was rewarded with increase, not just compensation.\n\nThe verse then explains the purpose behind this restoration: “as a mercy from Us and a lesson for people of reason.” The mercy is directly from Allah, emphasising that it was an unearned gift born of compassion. The lesson is for those with sound intellects, the ulū al-albāb, who reflect on the story and understand its implications. They learn that patience during trials never goes unrewarded, that relief is guaranteed after hardship, and that Allah’s mercy encompasses even the most severely tested. The story of Job becomes a living parable for every believer enduring sickness, poverty, or grief. It fortifies the heart with the conviction that if one remains steadfast without complaint against Allah, the end will be more beautiful than the beginning.\n\nThe mention of “twice as many” also connects to the earlier mention of David and Solomon’s gifts, showing that divine generosity knows no bounds when it is met with grateful patience.",
  "44": "This verse addresses a specific incident during Job’s trial. He had sworn an oath to strike his wife with a certain number of lashes if he recovered, likely because in a moment of extreme weakness she had suggested something inappropriate or had complained about their condition. When Allah restored him, Job was caught between his oath and his love and appreciation for his wife, who had faithfully served him throughout the illness. Allah, in His wisdom and mercy, provided a solution that upheld the oath without causing harm: “Take in your hand a bundle of grass, and strike your wife with it, and do not break your oath. ” A bundle of thin stalks or blades of grass, when struck all at once, counts as a single stroke yet fulfills the letter of an oath for multiple lashes without causing pain. This became an established principle in Islamic law for certain kinds of oaths, demonstrating that Allah desires ease and does not want to burden His servants with impossible situations.\n\nThe verse then gives Job a magnificent commendation: “We truly found him patient. What an excellent servant he was! Indeed, he constantly turned to Allah.” The patience was not passive; it was active and constant turning to Allah in every state.\n\nThe term awwāb again appears, linking Job to David and Solomon, and showing that the core of all these great servants was their perpetual return to their Lord. Job’s patience is so exemplary that his name became proverbial, and the Prophet Muhammad would often invoke his story to comfort those in distress.",
  "45": "After the detailed story of Job, the Quran briefly mentions other towering figures to reinforce the theme of remembering Allah’s chosen servants. “And remember Our servants: Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.” The three are mentioned together because they form the patriarchal line of the Israelite prophets. They are described as “the men of strength and insight. ” The Arabic ulī al-aydī wa al-abṣār literally means “possessors of hands and eyes.” “Hands” symbolise strength, capability, and powerful action in carrying out Allah’s commands and spreading the truth. Abraham’s strength was evident in his smashing of the idols and his readiness to sacrifice his son. Isaac and Jacob inherited this strength in upholding the covenant and guiding their families. “Eyes” refers to deep spiritual insight, the ability to see truth clearly amidst falsehood, and to perceive the realities of the unseen. They were not men of brute force alone; their actions were guided by profound wisdom and vision. This combination is the ideal of prophetic leadership: a balance of decisive action and deep contemplation.\n\nThe verse reminds the Prophet that these were “Our servants,” a title that elevates their servanthood above all worldly statuses. They were not kings in the conventional sense of the disbelievers’ thinking; they were servants of the Most High, and that was their real glory. Their example stands as a counterweight to the arrogance of the Quraysh chiefs, showing that true strength lies in submission to Allah.",
  "46": "This verse reveals the unique characteristic for which these great prophets were specially chosen. “We truly chose them for the honour of proclaiming the Hereafter.\n\n” The Arabic akhlaṣnāhum bi-khāliṣatin dhikrā ad-dār means that Allah purified and selected them with an exclusive quality: the constant remembrance of the final abode. Their entire mission was centred on reminding people of the Hereafter, the permanent home that awaits every soul.\n\nThis is the “honour” (khāliṣah) that distinguished them. Unlike worldly leaders who focus on accumulating power and riches, these prophets’ hearts were so immersed in the reality of the afterlife that it shaped every word they spoke and every action they took. They lived in this world but their souls were already anchored in the next.\n\nThe phrase “proclaiming the Hereafter” also implies that they made the Hereafter the core of their call, warning of its judgment and giving glad tidings of its bliss. This choice by Allah answers the disbelievers who mocked the idea of resurrection; the very prophets they claimed to respect were sent precisely to affirm that truth. For the believers, it is a reminder to orient their lives around the remembrance of the final home, making it the driving force behind their deeds. The Prophet Muhammad is now being shown that he shares this same honoured role with the patriarchs, and his focus on the Hereafter places him in their noble lineage.",
  "47": "The verse adds another layer of praise, affirming the permanent status of these prophets. “And in Our sight they are truly among the chosen and the finest.\n\n” The phrase “in Our sight” is profoundly intimate; it means that their rank is not determined by human opinion but by Allah’s direct knowledge and pleasure. They are “among the chosen” (al-muṣṭafayn), meaning Allah selected them from among all humanity for the highest mission, and they are “the finest” (al-akhyār), indicating that they are the best in character, conduct, and spiritual purity.\n\nThe word akhyār implies a superabundance of goodness, those who are inherently good and whose deeds are excellent. This commendation comes after describing their strength and their focus on the Hereafter, showing that these qualities are what make a person truly chosen and excellent in Allah’s eyes. It demolishes the false standards of the Quraysh, who measured excellence by lineage, wealth, and tribal power. Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob did not seek worldly prestige; they sought Allah’s approval, and that is what made them the finest. The verse serves as a powerful validation of the Prophet Muhammad’s mission, for he followed in their footsteps, and it encourages believers to aspire to be among Allah’s chosen and finest by adopting the same characteristics: strength in faith, deep insight, and a life oriented around the Hereafter.",
  "48": "The list of honoured prophets continues with three more names. “Also remember Ishmael, Elisha, and Dhul-Kifl.” Ishmael is the firstborn son of Abraham, the ancestor of the Arabs, and the one who helped his father build the Kaaba. His inclusion here ties the Arabian Prophet directly to this blessed lineage. Elisha (al-Yasaʿ) was a prophet sent to the Children of Israel, a successor to Elijah, known for his miracles and steadfastness. Dhul-Kifl is mentioned only twice in the Quran, and scholars offer various identifications, often suggesting he was a righteous prophet or a saintly man known for his commitment to fulfilling a great responsibility.\n\nThe name literally means “the one of the fold” or “possessor of a double portion,” and he is praised for his patience and righteousness. The verse concludes, “All are among the best. ” The simple but powerful phrase emphasises that these prophets were not just good; they were among the very best of creation. By commanding the remembrance of these figures, the Quran provides the Prophet with a spiritual support system. In times of rejection and difficulty, recalling the lives of these towering servants—their trials, their patience, their unwavering faith—fills the heart with strength and perspective. The diversity of their stories, from the patriarchs to a figure like Dhul-Kifl, shows that the path of righteousness is walked by many, each with unique tests, but all sharing the common destination of Allah’s pleasure.",
  "49": "The narrative now reaches a transitional point. “This is all a reminder.\n\n” The phrase encompasses everything that has been mentioned so far: the stories of the prophets, the proofs of monotheism, the warnings to the disbelievers, and the descriptions of blessings and trials. All of it serves as a powerful reminder for those who wish to be reminded. Then the focus shifts to the ultimate outcome, contrasting the reward of the righteous with the punishment of the wicked, which forms the remainder of the surah. “And the righteous will certainly have an honourable destination.” The righteous, the muttaqūn, are those who lived in constant consciousness of Allah, guarding themselves against sin and fulfilling their duties. Their destination is described as “honourable” (ḥusna maʾāb), a beautiful and excellent place of return.\n\nThe word maʾāb implies a place one returns to again and again, a permanent home. It is honourable because it is a place of dignity, peace, and divine pleasure, a stark contrast to the humiliation and disgrace awaiting the transgressors. This verse sets the stage for the detailed descriptions of Paradise that follow. It assures the believers that no matter how difficult the journey in this world becomes, the final return is one of honour, prepared by the Most Generous for those who remained true.",
  "50": "The honourable destination is now named: “the Gardens of Eternity,” ʿAdn, denoting permanence and perpetual residence. Unlike the transient dwellings of the world, these gardens will never wither, and their inhabitants will never leave.\n\nThe first striking image of this welcome is that “whose gates will be open for them.” In the world, even the most magnificent palaces have guarded gates that open only for select visitors after scrutiny. But for the righteous, the gates of Paradise are already wide open, ready to receive them with honour. The angels will greet them with peace, and there will be no barriers, no waiting, no fear of rejection. The openness of the gates symbolises complete acceptance and the removal of all obstacles between them and their Lord’s mercy. It also reflects a state of eternal security; the gates never close because there is no danger, no night, and no need for protection.\n\nThe image evokes a feeling of joyous arrival: the long journey of faith through a world of trials ends with an entrance that is effortless and welcoming. This is a direct answer to the earlier verses about the disbelievers who arrogantly rejected the call. For them, the gates of Hell will be slammed shut; for the righteous, the gates of Paradise will be flung open, never to close.",
  "51": "The scene inside the Gardens is painted with a few masterful strokes. “There they will recline.” Reclining is the posture of complete ease, security, and contentment, the opposite of the toil and hardship of earthly life. They will be reclining on sumptuous couches, surrounded by every comfort. From this state of perfect rest, they will “call for abundant fruit and drink.\n\n” The word “calling” (yadʿūn) suggests that they merely need to express a wish or indicate a desire, and it will be immediately fulfilled by servants or brought to them without delay. The “abundant fruit” means fruits of every variety, plentiful and never out of season, without any effort of cultivation or harvest. The “drink” refers to pure, delightful beverages that cause no intoxication or harm. The entire image is one of royal hospitality provided by the Lord of all worlds. There is no cooking, no cleaning, no labour; every desire is met with supernatural ease.\n\nThis description is a powerful motivation for the believers enduring scarcity and persecution in Makkah. It tells them that their patience will be rewarded with a life where longing does not exist, because everything one could ever want is available at a word. The contrast with the boiling water and pus that the wicked will taste, mentioned later, could not be sharper. The righteous call for delights; the wicked are forced to swallow torment.",
  "52": "Among the blessings of Paradise, the Quran mentions the companions of its inhabitants. “And with them will be maidens of modest gaze and equal age.\n\n” The phrase “maidens of modest gaze” translates ḥūr ʿīn: ḥūr refers to those with intensely white eyes and deep black pupils, a mark of extraordinary beauty, and ʿīn describes wide, beautiful eyes. But their physical beauty is paired with a moral quality: “modest gaze” (qaṣirāt aṭ-ṭarf), meaning they gaze only at their spouses with love and fidelity, never looking at anyone else with desire. There is a purity and exclusivity in this relationship that surpasses anything known on earth. “Equal age” (atrāb) means they are all of the same youthful, vibrant age, enjoying one another’s company in perfect harmony. These maidens are not mere objects of desire; they are pure, loving companions created for an eternal, faithful union.\n\nThis description countered the pagan Arabs’ notions of sensuality by elevating it to a spiritual, purified form within the context of everlasting joy. It also serves as a comfort for the believing men and women who maintained chastity and fidelity in the world, promising them a reward that is free from the jealousy, betrayal, and imperfection of earthly relationships.\n\nThe mention of “with them” indicates that these companions are part of the overall atmosphere of delight, not the sole focus. The entire setting is one of complete, wholesome felicity where every relationship is a source of peace and joy.",
  "53": "After detailing the blessings, the Quran directly addresses the believers, anchoring these descriptions in certainty. “This is what you are promised for the Day of Reckoning.\n\n” The phrase “Day of Reckoning” (Yawm al-Ḥisāb) is a weighty reminder that all of this is not a fantasy but a guaranteed outcome of a real event. There will be a day when every soul will be called to account, deeds weighed, and judgment pronounced. It is on that day, after the reckoning, that the righteous will enter into this described bliss. The promise is Allah’s word, and Allah never breaks His promise. This verse connects the beautiful imagery back to the surah’s earlier theme: the disbelievers denied the Day of Reckoning and mocked it, but the believers accept it and orient their lives around it. The promise serves as a source of immense hope and patience. When the world inflicts pain or when one must give up immediate pleasures for the sake of Allah, remembering that this is the promised reward for the Day of Reckoning makes every sacrifice worthwhile. It is a transaction where the fleeting is exchanged for the eternal.\n\nThe verse also implies a subtle warning: if this is the promise for the Day of Reckoning, what will be the reality of that day for those who denied it? The answer follows immediately in the description of the wicked’s fate.",
  "54": "The final seal on the description of Paradise is a declaration of its eternal nature. “This is indeed Our provision that will never end.\n\n” The word “provision” (rizq) is deeply significant. In this world, provision is always temporary; the best meal is digested, the most beautiful garden withers, and every joy fades. Human anxiety is often driven by the fear that provision will run out. But Allah states that this provision in Paradise is His provision, and it will never end. It will never diminish, never spoil, never be withdrawn. There is no depletion, no boredom, no limit.\n\nThe Arabic mā lahu min nafād means it has absolutely no exhaustion or depletion. This is the ultimate security: not only is the punishment avoided, but the reward is a perpetual, uninterrupted flow of blessings from the Lord of infinite bounty. This verse directly answers the disbelievers’ earlier arrogant question about who possesses the treasuries of mercy.\n\nThe answer is Allah, and He opens those treasuries without measure for His righteous servants. It also links back to Solomon’s story, where he was told, “This is Our gift, so give or withhold as you wish.” In Paradise, the gift is even greater: a provision that is eternally available and never withdrawn. The verse brings the description of the righteous’ destination to a close, setting it as an unshakeable hope for every believer.",
  "55": "A sharp and deliberate contrast is drawn. “That is that.\n\n” The phrase summarizes the preceding description of the reward as settled and decisive. Then the attention turns to the other group: “And the transgressors will certainly have the worst destination. ” The transgressors are the ṭāghūn, those who exceed all bounds in rebellion, arrogance, and sin. They are the opposite of the righteous. Their destination is described as “the worst” (sharra maʾāb), a vile and dreadful place of return.\n\nThe use of maʾāb again, paralleling the honourable destination, underscores the symmetry of justice: the righteous return to honour, the wicked return to disgrace. The verse creates a stark cliff in the mind of the listener, preparing for the terrifying description that follows. It mirrors the earlier verse that posed the rhetorical question: should the righteous be treated like the wicked?\n\nThe answer is now given in vivid detail. This structure—the beautiful reward first, then the horrifying punishment—is typical of the Quranic method of instilling hope and fear in balanced measure. The believer’s heart should oscillate between longing for Paradise and fear of Hell, both motivating righteous action.\n\nThe word “certainly” (la) in the Arabic adds an emphasis of inevitability: just as the righteous will surely enter their bliss, the transgressors will surely enter their doom.",
  "56": "The worst destination is now explicitly named: “Hell,” Jahannam. The description that follows is brutally direct: “where they will burn.” The verb yaṣlawnahā means they will be thrown into it, roasted in its flames, enduring its intense heat. There is no metaphorical softening; it is a literal, excruciating experience. The fire will envelop them from all sides.\n\nThe verse then adds an exclamation of profound disgust and warning: “What an evil place to rest!” The word “rest” translates mihād, which can mean a bed, a resting place, or a cradle. In worldly terms, a person’s bed is a place of comfort and security. But in Hell, the bed will be fire, and the blankets will be fire. The irony is sharp: the transgressors sought their rest and comfort in sin, and now their eternal resting place is the very torment they mocked. The exclamation is not an external comment but a divine verdict meant to pierce the heart of the listener. It echoes the earlier “woe” to the disbelievers. The verse removes any ambiguity: Hell is not a temporary rehabilitation centre; it is an evil resting place, a permanent abode of agony for those who rejected faith and persisted in wrongdoing.\n\nThis is the reality that the Prophet was sent to warn against, and the weight of that warning hangs over every verse.",
  "57": "The punishment is not limited to burning; it includes the vile things they will be forced to consume. “Let them then taste this: boiling water and oozing pus.” The command “taste” is laced with a tone of just recompense; they are being made to experience the fruits of their deeds.\n\nThe first item is ḥamīm, boiling water that has reached an extreme temperature. When brought near their faces, it scalds them; when they attempt to drink it, it tears their insides apart. It is the ultimate antithesis of the cool, refreshing spring given to Job.\n\nThe second item is ghassāq, translated as “oozing pus.” It refers to the foul discharge that flows from the bodies of the inhabitants of Hell, a mixture of blood, pus, and melted skin. It is described elsewhere as intensely cold or having a nauseating stench, adding layers of agony.\n\nThis is what will be offered as drink and food to the transgressors. The contrast with the abundant fruit and pure drink of Paradise is unimaginable. In the world, they indulged in luxurious food and drink, never thanking the Provider; in Hell, their provision is this torment. The verb “taste” indicates a direct, personal experience. They will not merely see it; it will be forced upon them. This verse serves as a severe deterrent, making the listener recoil and flee to the mercy of Allah, seeking the provision that never ends instead.",
  "58": "The verse intensifies the horror by stating that these two terrible things are not the only ones. “And other torments of the same sort!\n\n” The Arabic means that they will face punishments paired with them, of similar severity and in various forms. Hell contains multiple types of agony: chains, shackles, fire, boiling water, pus, thorny food, bitter fruit, and a scorching wind. All of these are “of the same sort,” meaning they are equally punishing and equally loathsome.\n\nThe pairing creates a sense of being overwhelmed; relief never comes because if one torment subsides, another of the same kind takes its place. There is no moment of respite.\n\nThe phrase also implies that every sin has a fitting punishment, and the variety of torments corresponds to the variety of their transgressions. This is a manifestation of perfect justice. The verse does not list all the torments, leaving the imagination to grasp that the reality is far worse than words can convey. The believer, upon hearing this, is filled with a sense of urgency to avoid anything that could lead to such a fate. The surah has moved from the patience of Job to the ultimate consequences, driving home that the trials of this world, if borne with faith, lead to eternal ease, while the pleasures of sin lead to eternal suffering.",
  "59": "The scene now shifts to the interactions among the damned, exposing their mutual hatred. The misleaders are the chiefs and the arrogant leaders who called people to disbelief. When they see their followers being driven into Hell alongside them, they exclaim, “Here is a crowd of followers being thrown in with us.\n\n” The phrase “being thrown in” highlights the violent, humiliating manner of their entry. Instead of showing any remnant of comradeship, the misleaders immediately declare, “They are not welcome, for they too will burn in the Fire.” In the world, these leaders loved the adulation and blind following of the masses, but now that following brings no benefit; it only increases the crowd in the Fire. Their statement that the followers are “not welcome” is a curse of mutual repulsion. There is no honour among the wicked in the Hereafter. The shared suffering does not create solidarity; it creates enmity.\n\nThis is a piercing lesson for those who follow others blindly in matters of faith. On the Day of Judgment, the false leaders will disown their followers, and the followers will disown the leaders. The bonds of misguidance will turn into instruments of torment. The verse starkly fulfills the earlier warning that the disbelievers are an enemy force bound for defeat; their unity in sin becomes their joint destruction, with no comfort in numbers.",
  "60": "The followers, far from accepting the insult, reply with their own curse. “No! You are not welcome!” They return the same expression of rejection, negating any greeting of peace. Then they add the reason for their curse: “You brought this upon us.” They recognize that the misleaders were the primary cause of their doom because they beautified disbelief, misrepresented the truth, and blocked the path of guidance.\n\nThis is a moment of painful clarity: all the excuses vanish, and the reality of responsibility is laid bare. The followers admit that they were misled, but their admission does not absolve them; it simply increases their anguish. They end with the same type of exclamation used earlier: “What an evil place for settlement!\n\n” The word qarār, translated as “settlement,” implies a permanent dwelling. Hell is not just a passing punishment; it is their final, fixed abode of misery. The dispute between the two groups will continue, each blaming the other, but the torment will not be lightened.\n\nThis verse teaches the critical importance of personal accountability in faith. One cannot rely on one’s family, leaders, or society to bear the burden of misguidance. Each soul must seek the truth for itself, and those who refuse to use their reason, as the surah repeatedly urges, will find no allies in the Fire, only enemies sharing the same fate.",
  "61": "After the followers have cursed their misleaders and declared them unwelcome, they turn their faces upward with a desperate prayer. They cry out, “Our Lord! Whoever brought this upon us, double their punishment in the Fire.\n\n” The phrase “brought this upon us” refers to the one who first initiated the call to misguidance, the original architect of the disbelief and rebellion that led them all into Hell. The followers recognise that while they themselves were complicit, there is a primary culprit who beautified the sin and led them step by step into destruction. Their request for a double punishment is both an expression of their intense hatred and a profound Quranic principle: the one who leads others astray bears the sin of his own misguidance and a share of the sins of those who followed him, without diminishing the followers’ own burdens in any way. The Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, said, “Whoever calls to guidance will have a reward similar to the rewards of those who follow him, without that diminishing their rewards in any way. And whoever calls to misguidance will have a burden of sin similar to the burdens of those who follow him, without that diminishing their burdens in any way. ” This verse is the living enactment of that hadith. The followers, now fully aware that their leaders deceived them, do not ask for their own punishment to be lightened; they seek vengeance against the cause of their ruin.\n\nYet this prayer will not avail them, for each soul is recompensed for what it earned, and the double portion is already decreed for those who combined their own disbelief with the active misleading of others. The verse shows that the social bonds of sin in this world become the chains of mutual enmity in the Hereafter. The friends who encouraged each other to mock the prophets and to deny the resurrection will become the bitterest of enemies, each wishing worse torment upon the other. The lesson for the living is stark: be careful whom you follow, and be even more careful about the influence you exert on others, for leadership in sin is a direct path to multiplied agony.",
  "62": "The scene deepens as the disbelieving leaders, the tyrants who considered themselves the elite, begin to look around the Fire. They notice something that disturbs them greatly: the absence of certain people. They ask one another, “But why do we not see those we considered to be lowly?\n\n” The word “lowly” translates the Arabic arādhil, a term of contempt that the Quraysh chiefs frequently used for the poor and socially marginalized believers like Bilāl, ʿAmmār, Khabbāb, and Suhayb. In Makkah, the elite regarded themselves as the people of substance and honour, while they dismissed the early Muslims as the weak, the slaves, and the insignificant. They would ask the Prophet to drive these believers away if he wanted the nobles to sit with him. Now, in the blazing Fire, they instinctively expect to see those same lowly believers suffering alongside them. Their arrogance was so deeply ingrained that they assumed the poor believers could not possibly have a better fate. The fact that they do not see them creates a moment of jarring confusion. Some scholars explain that the tyrants are scanning the pits of Hell, searching for the believers among the tortured masses, and when they fail to locate them, a horrifying possibility creeps into their minds. Could it be that those whom they ridiculed are actually in Paradise, enjoying the very delights they themselves were promised if only they had believed? This verse is a divine reversal of worldly standards. The ones the world looked down upon are raised high, and the ones who strutted with pride are plunged low. The absence of the believers from the Fire is a silent but thunderous testimony to the truth of the message that the weak accepted and the strong rejected. It confirms the earlier principle that Allah does not treat the righteous like the wicked.",
  "63": "The tyrants grapple with two possible explanations for the missing believers. Their first speculation is, “Were we wrong in mocking them in the world?\n\n” This is a glimmer of self-awareness, a crack in their wall of arrogance. They begin to suspect that their entire evaluation was inverted; the people they ridiculed as worthless were in fact the treasured servants of Allah. Their mockery, which they had wielded as a weapon of social exclusion, now returns to haunt them as a source of deep regret.\n\nThe word “wrong” could also imply that they were deluded or astray in their assessment. This is a profound Quranic insight: mockery of the righteous is not just a social sin but an intellectual error, a failure to perceive true value.\n\nThe second speculation reveals how hard it is for arrogance to fully die, even in Hell. They ask, “Or do our eyes just fail to see them in the Fire?” Perhaps, they think, the believers are somewhere in the deeper, darker pits, and the smoke and the flames obscure their vision. This assumption is a desperate attempt to preserve their old worldview, to avoid the shattering conclusion that the lowly ones are actually in bliss. The fact that both possibilities are presented shows the psychological torment of the damned: they oscillate between a dawning recognition of their error and a stubborn refusal to accept the full truth. It is a state of cognitive dissonance that magnifies their suffering.\n\nThe verse teaches that the consequences of worldly arrogance extend into the next life, where the truth becomes undeniable but can no longer benefit the one who denied it. The mockers will learn, beyond all doubt, that the scales of divine judgment bear no resemblance to the social hierarchies of the world.",
  "64": "Having portrayed the back-and-forth of curses, blame, and bewildered searching among the people of Hell, the Quran now seals this scene with a powerful statement of certainty. “This dispute between the residents of the Fire will certainly come to pass.\n\n” The Arabic emphasizes the word “dispute” (takhaṣum), which implies a vehement, back-and-forth argument. It is not a quiet conversation but a heated altercation in which each party tries to shift blame, express hatred, and seek some form of twisted satisfaction through the condemnation of others.\n\nThe phrase “will certainly come to pass” is absolute; there is no doubt or metaphor here. It is a factual event as real as the Fire itself. This verse bridges the detailed descriptions of the afterlife with the overall objective of the surah: to serve as a reminder. The Prophet is being told to convey this scene with conviction, because it is as true as the world they walk upon.\n\nFor the disbelievers who mock the idea of a Day of Reckoning, this verse is a direct refutation. Their denial does not make the event any less real. For the believers, it is both a warning and a comfort: a warning against following the arrogant, and a comfort that the mockery they endured from the tyrants will be answered with the perfect justice of that Day.\n\nThe verse also prepares the listener for the shift that follows, where the Prophet is instructed to reassert the core of his message in the face of all the denial, anchoring the entire discussion in the worship of the One True God.",
  "65": "After the graphic portrayal of the afterlife, the Prophet is commanded to declare his mission with absolute clarity. “Say, O Prophet, ‘I am only a warner.’” The word “only” restricts his role and demolishes any false accusations. He is not a magician, a poet, a power-seeker, or someone who fabricates revelation. He is strictly a warner, a nadhīr, sent to alert people to a danger that is approaching and to give them the opportunity to prepare. This echoes the earlier verse where the disbelievers were astonished that a warner came from among themselves. Now the Prophet reaffirms that this is precisely what he is, nothing more and nothing less. Immediately following this self-definition, he delivers the core content of his warning: “And there is no god worthy of worship except Allah—the One, the Supreme.” The declaration of tawḥīd is the foundation upon which the entire message stands. Allah is described with two majestic names: al-Wāḥid, the One, unique and indivisible in His essence, attributes, and right to be worshipped; and al-Qahhār, the Supreme, the Subduer, the One who overpowers all and to whom everything submits.\n\nThe pairing is significant. The One God is not a remote, indifferent deity; He is the Supreme Dominant who will hold all creation to account. This directly counters the Meccan idolaters who associated partners with Allah and yet felt secure from His punishment. It also links back to the stories of David, Solomon, Job, and the other prophets, who all called to the worship of this One Supreme God. The verse is a reset, bringing the focus back to the central truth after the terrifying depictions of the Fire, leaving the listener with a clear choice: heed the warner and submit to the One Supreme God, or continue in arrogance and face the consequences.",
  "66": "The declaration of Allah’s oneness and supremacy is immediately grounded in His absolute ownership and power. “He is the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between.\n\n” The word “Lord” (Rabb) encompasses the Creator, Owner, Sustainer, and Master. Everything that exists, from the highest heavens to the deepest earth and all that lies in the vast spaces in between, belongs to Him, is sustained by Him, and operates under His command. This echoes the earlier rhetorical challenge posed to the disbelievers: “Or is the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything in between theirs?\n\n” The answer is now emphatically given: it belongs to Allah alone. Since He is the Lord of all, He has the sole right to be worshipped and to issue commands. Then the verse couples two more names that balance fear and hope: “the Almighty, Most Forgiving. ” Al-ʿAzīz is the Almighty, the One whose might cannot be resisted, who enforces His will with irresistible power. This name should strike awe into the heart of the rebel, for no one can escape His grasp. But immediately next to it is al-Ghaffār, the Most Forgiving, the One who repeatedly and abundantly forgives the sins of those who turn to Him in repentance. This pairing is a profound mercy. The same Lord who will crush the arrogant tyrants in the Fire is the Lord who eagerly forgives the repentant sinner. No one should despair of His mercy, but no one should take His might lightly. The verse provides the complete framework for a relationship with Allah: a recognition of His absolute ownership, a fear of His irresistible power, and a hope in His boundless forgiveness. The warner’s message is not one of doom alone; it is an invitation to seek the forgiveness of the Almighty before the Day when His might will be manifested in judgment.",
  "67": "The Prophet is commanded to make a third declaration, this time about the nature of the revelation itself. “Say, ‘This Quran is momentous news.’” The word translated as “momentous” is ʿaẓīm, meaning great, tremendous, weighty. The Quran is described as nabaʾ ʿaẓīm, a piece of news of immense significance. What is this news? It is the news of the resurrection, the judgment, Paradise and Hell, the ultimate triumph of truth and the crushing defeat of falsehood. It is the news that the entire course of human history is moving towards a single, decisive Day of Reckoning. This news is not trivial gossip or ancient legend; it is the most important information a human being can ever receive, because it determines one’s eternal fate. The description of the Quran as “news” is also striking. It is not a work of philosophy or a collection of abstract ideas; it is a report about reality—about Allah, the unseen, the past nations, and the coming future. The Prophet is merely the bearer of this news; his duty is to deliver it faithfully. This framing responds directly to the disbelievers who accused him of fabricating the Quran. How can he fabricate news of such magnitude, details of the unseen that no human could know, and scenes of the afterlife that are described with such precision and consistency?\n\nThe word “momentous” also hints at the Quran’s effect: it is a heavy, serious message that shakes the conscience and demands a response. No one can truly hear this news and remain indifferent. One either accepts it with awe and preparation, or one turns away in arrogant dismissal, as the next verse describes.",
  "68": "The tragedy is that this momentous news, which should command the full attention of every human soul, is being met with deliberate neglect. “From which you pagans are turning away.\n\n” The Arabic verb muʿriḍūn implies a persistent, active avoidance. They are not merely ignorant; the news has reached them through the Prophet’s clear recitation, yet they turn their faces, change the subject, and warn one another not to listen. The surah earlier recorded their leaders going forth and saying, “Carry on, and stand firm in devotion to your gods.” That is the very embodiment of turning away. This turning away is not a passive state; it requires a conscious effort to suppress the natural inclination of the heart to respond to the truth. The pagans busied themselves with distractions, mockery, and arguments precisely to avoid confronting the weight of the Quranic message. The verse contains an implicit warning: turning away from momentous news does not make it disappear. A person who receives a dire warning and ignores it does not neutralize the danger; they merely walk into it unprepared. The pagans’ turning away is a self-inflicted harm.\n\nThe verse also offers a subtle psychological insight: the very act of describing the Quran as “momentous news” while observing that they turn away exposes the irrationality of their behaviour. Why would anyone turn away from news of supreme importance about their own eternal destiny unless they were blinded by arrogance or enslaved by desires?\n\nThe question hangs in the air, inviting every listener to examine their own response to the Quran.",
  "69": "The Prophet is instructed to add another crucial point that proves his prophethood. “I had no knowledge of the highest assembly in heaven when they differed concerning Adam.” The “highest assembly” refers to al-malaʾ al-aʿlā, the exalted gathering of the angels. The “differing” concerning Adam refers to the discussion that took place among the angels when Allah announced His intention to place a vicegerent on earth. The angels, knowing the potential of humans to cause bloodshed and corruption, questioned this decision in a spirit of seeking understanding, not objection. Then there was the subsequent command for them to prostrate to Adam, and the refusal of Iblīs. This entire narrative is part of the unseen, events that took place before human history in the heavenly realm. The Prophet Muhammad was an unlettered man living in Makkah, with no access to the scriptures of the Jews and Christians, and no human teacher who could have informed him of these details. By stating that he had no knowledge of this before revelation, he is presenting a clear proof that the Quran is from Allah. The only way he could narrate this story with such accuracy and authority is through divine revelation. This verse prepares the ground for the detailed account of Adam and Iblīs that follows. It is a direct challenge to anyone who doubts the source of the Quran: if the Prophet had no natural means of knowing this, and yet he recites it, the only remaining explanation is that the Knower of the unseen has informed him.\n\nThe verse also links the story to the overall theme of the surah: the origin of human enmity with Satan, which explains the root of the arrogance and rejection the Prophet is currently facing.",
  "70": "The verse concludes the introductory statements before the narrative of Adam. “What is revealed to me is that I am only sent with a clear warning.” The Prophet reiterates that the entirety of the revelation he receives is oriented around a single mission: to serve as a clear warner.\n\nThe phrase “clear warning” translates nadhīr mubīn, a warning that leaves no ambiguity, backed by evident proofs and plain language. The knowledge of the highest assembly, the story of the angels and Adam, and all the other unseen news are not given to him for his personal prestige or to satisfy curiosity. They are all part of the warning, designed to awaken the heart and illuminate the reality of the human condition.\n\nThe mention of “only” again strips away any pretension: he is not claiming to be an angel, a lord, or a possessor of Allah’s treasuries; he is a man who received a message and must deliver it. This humility is the hallmark of true prophethood.\n\nThe verse also connects back to the very beginning of the surah, where the pagans were astonished that a warner came from among them. The Prophet is simply fulfilling that divinely assigned role. The “clear warning” encompasses everything that has come before in the surah: the destruction of past nations, the account of the prophets, the description of Paradise and Hell, and now the story of the primal act of disobedience. All of it serves as a mirror in which every human being can see the consequences of their choices. The stage is now set for the dramatic account of the first act of arrogance against Allah’s command.",
  "71": "The Quran now transports us back to the moment before human history began, instructing the Prophet to remember and convey this foundational event. “Remember, O Prophet, when your Lord said to the angels.” The address “your Lord” is deeply personal to the Prophet, reminding him of his close connection to the source of this revelation. The audience is the angels, the noble beings of light who ceaselessly obey Allah and surround His throne. The announcement is momentous: “I am going to create a human being from clay.” The original word for “human being” is bashar, which refers to the physical, mortal creature made of tangible material.\n\nThe mention of “clay” grounds this new creation in the earth. Unlike the angels who were created from light and the jinn from smokeless fire, the human being’s origin is humble, from the dust and mud of the ground.\n\nYet this very humility would become the vessel for something extraordinary: a soul breathed directly from Allah, and the capacity for knowledge, free will, and moral choice. The announcement is a declaration of intent, not a consultation. Allah informs the angels of what He will do, and the angels, in another surah, respond with a question born of their desire to understand: “Will You place therein one who will spread corruption and shed blood, while we glorify You with Your praise and sanctify You?” Their question shows their awareness of the potential dark side of a creature with free will. This verse, however, focuses solely on the divine decree to create, highlighting that the creation of humanity is a deliberate, purposeful act of the Almighty. The human being is not an accident, but a chosen creation with a specific role in the cosmic order.",
  "72": "The creation process is described in two distinct stages. First, Allah says, “So when I have fashioned him.” This refers to the physical formation of Adam’s body from clay, shaped into perfect human form by the direct power of Allah.\n\nThe word “fashioned” (sawwaytu) implies a balanced, proportionate, and complete creation, free of any defect. The second stage is the infusion of life: “and had a spirit of My Own creation breathed into him.” The “spirit” is the rūḥ, the soul, the divine breath that transformed the clay figure into a living, conscious being.\n\nThe phrase “of My Own creation” indicates that this spirit is a special creation attributed directly to Allah as an honour, though it is not a part of Allah Himself; it is a created entity that belongs to Him in a unique way, being the vehicle of consciousness, intellect, and moral capacity. This breathing of the spirit is the moment of the human being’s distinction. The clay body connects humanity to the earth, but the spirit connects them to the divine command. It is this combination of humble origin and lofty soul that defines the human condition. Then comes the command to the angels: “fall down in prostration to him.” This prostration was not an act of worship, for worship is due only to Allah. It was a prostration of greeting, honour, and acknowledgment of Adam’s superiority in knowledge and his role as vicegerent. The entire heavenly assembly was commanded to bow before this new creation, a profound sign of the honour Allah bestowed upon humanity. This moment represents the first test of obedience in relation to the human being, and it would separate the faithful angels from the arrogant Iblīs.",
  "73": "The response of the angels was immediate, unanimous, and complete. “So the angels prostrated all together.” The verse emphasises that there was no hesitation, no partial obedience, and no delay.\n\nThe word “all together” translates ajmaʿūn, meaning every single one of them, as a unified collective, carried out the command. This is the nature of the angels: they are created to obey. They do not question the wisdom of Allah’s commands once His will is made clear. Their prostration was a magnificent display of humility before the divine decree, even though Adam was made of clay and they were made of light. They did not look at the material origin and deem themselves superior; they looked at the Creator’s command and submitted. This unanimous prostration sets the stage for the shock of the one refusal that follows. It creates a backdrop of perfect obedience against which the rebellion of Iblīs becomes starkly visible. The angels’ action also signified their acceptance of the human being’s elevated status in Allah’s plan, despite their earlier questions about potential corruption. They trusted that Allah’s wisdom in creating Adam was perfect. This verse implicitly teaches the believers the proper response to Allah’s commands: swift, complete, and free from the arrogance that would compare one’s own perceived status to what Allah has ordered. The prostration of the angels is also a prefiguration of the honour that the righteous descendants of Adam will receive in Paradise, where they will be served and honoured by angels.",
  "74": "In the midst of that unified prostration, one being stood apart. “But not Iblīs.” The exception is stark and solitary. Iblīs, also known as Satan, was a jinn who had, through his intense devotion and worship, been elevated to the company of the angels. He was not an angel by nature—the Quran clarifies elsewhere that he was of the jinn—but he had reached a rank where he was addressed alongside them. When the command came, his inner reality was exposed. The verse describes his crime in two stages: “who acted arrogantly, becoming unfaithful.\n\n” The word “arrogantly” (istakbara) indicates that he considered himself too great to bow before Adam. This was the first sin in creation: pride. He looked at Adam’s origin of clay and compared it to his own origin of fire, and in his heart, a sense of superiority took root. This arrogance then led directly to “becoming unfaithful.\n\n” The Arabic kāna min al-kāfirīn means he became one of those who cover up and reject the truth. Iblīs had knowledge of Allah’s command, yet he willfully disobeyed it. His act was not a slip or a mistake; it was a conscious, prideful rejection that defined his new identity. He moved from being a devout worshipper to being the embodiment of rebellion. This transformation is a timeless warning. Arrogance is the direct path to kufr, disbelief. No matter how much a person has worshipped, if they allow pride to enter their heart and refuse to submit to a clear command of Allah, they can fall from the highest station to the lowest.\n\nThe verse also exposes the root of all the rejection faced by the Prophet: it is the same Iblīs-like arrogance that prevents the Quraysh chiefs from bowing to the truth brought by a man from among themselves. The story of Iblīs is the archetype of every subsequent act of rebellion against divine guidance.",
  "75": "Allah, in His knowledge, already knew the answer, but He questioned Iblīs to make the crime explicit and to establish the just basis for the punishment. The question begins with a direct address: “O Iblīs! What prevented you from prostrating to what I created with My Own Hands?\n\n” The phrase “with My Own Hands” is immensely significant. It denotes special honour and direct attention given to Adam’s creation. While all creation comes into being by Allah’s command “Be,” Adam’s formation is uniquely attributed to Allah’s hands, signifying care, nobility, and a personal involvement that elevates Adam above other creatures. By refusing to prostrate to a being that Allah Himself had so honoured, Iblīs was directly insulting the divine choice.\n\nThe question then exposes the inner state of Iblīs with two piercing options: “Did you just become proud? Or have you always been arrogant?\n\n” The first option, “Did you just become proud?” implies a new, emerging arrogance that led to this specific act. The second, “Or have you always been arrogant?” suggests that pride was a deep-seated, permanent trait that was now being manifested. In either case, the arrogance is condemned.\n\nThe question is a form of reprimand and a revelation of the absurdity of his stance. Iblīs was not able to claim that the command was unclear or that Adam was unworthy. His refusal had no basis in justice or reason; it was purely the ugly head of pride rising up against the wisdom of the Creator.\n\nThis verse teaches that Allah examines not just the act of disobedience but its root cause in the heart. Arrogance is particularly heinous because it implies that a creature knows better than the Creator, or that the creature’s judgment of worth is more valid than Allah’s.",
  "76": "Iblīs’s response is the classic justification of the arrogant. He does not deny the command; he simply declares his own superiority. “I am better than he is: You created me from fire and him from clay.\n\n” This is the first qiyās, the first act of flawed analogical reasoning in creation. Iblīs compared two elements—fire and clay—and assigned a value hierarchy to them. Fire is upward-leaping, luminous, and energetic; clay is lowly, dark, and passive. Based on this material comparison, he concluded that a creature made of fire must be superior to one made of clay. But his reasoning was corrupt on multiple levels. First, superiority is not determined by raw materials but by piety, obedience, and the honour that Allah bestows. A palace of gold can be a den of evil, while a hut of mud can house a saint. Second, he completely ignored that the clay was fashioned by Allah’s own hands and infused with His spirit, while the fire from which he was made had no such unique honour mentioned. Third, his arrogance blinded him to the fact that Allah had commanded him to prostrate; regardless of his opinion about clay and fire, the command of the Creator nullifies all such comparisons. Iblīs’s logic is the root of all racism, caste systems, and class prejudice. When a person says, “I am better than another because of my skin colour, my lineage, my wealth, or my nationality,” they are echoing the satanic reasoning. The Prophet Muhammad later explicitly demolished this mindset when he said, “O people, verily your Lord is One and your father is one. There is no superiority of an Arab over a non-Arab, nor of a non-Arab over an Arab, nor of a white over a black, nor of a black over a white, except through taqwā. ” Iblīs failed the test precisely because he valued matter over the command of Allah, and in doing so, he became the father of all such error.",
  "77": "The response of Iblīs sealed his fate. There was no room for further argument or opportunity for repentance. Allah commanded, “Then get out of Paradise.\n\n” The word “get out” (ukhruj) is a decree of expulsion, a forceful ejection from the abode of honour and peace. Iblīs, who had been dwelling in the company of the angels in the celestial realm, was now cast down. Paradise, in this context, refers to the heavenly gardens above, not the Eternal Paradise of the Hereafter, but a realm of divine proximity from which he was now banished. The reason for this expulsion is given immediately: “for you are truly cursed.” The curse is laʿnah, which means being expelled and distanced from the mercy of Allah. It is not merely a word of anger; it is a state of being spiritually outcast, with no hope of redemption.\n\nThe word “truly” adds emphasis that this curse is a settled, permanent reality. The arrogance of a single moment led to an eternal loss. This verse demonstrates the decisive nature of divine judgment when the inner reality of a creature is made manifest. Iblīs had enjoyed a long period of worship, yet when his hidden pride was exposed through the test of Adam, all that past worship could not save him. It is a chilling reminder that the end of deeds matters most, and that a hidden disease of the heart, if not rooted out, can undo a lifetime of apparent piety. The expulsion of Iblīs is also the beginning of the cosmic struggle between the forces of satanic pride and the children of Adam, setting the stage for the test of human life on earth.",
  "78": "The curse is not a temporary state; it is extended and fixed. “And surely upon you is My condemnation until the Day of Judgment.\n\n” The word “condemnation” translates laʿnatī, My curse. The possessive “My” adds a terrifying weight; it is not the curse of a mere creation but the curse of the Almighty Creator, the only One whose curse carries ultimate effect. This condemnation will remain upon Iblīs continuously from that moment until the Day of Judgment, the day when all beings will be resurrected for the final reckoning. This means that for the entire span of human history, Iblīs is an accursed being, operating under divine wrath.\n\nThe phrase “Day of Judgment” (Yawm ad-Dīn) ties the story directly to the central theme of the surah. Iblīs’s respite and his mission of misguidance are all contained within this timeframe. On the Day of Judgment, the curse will manifest in its fullest form as he and his followers are cast into the Fire. The verse provides a crucial piece of the cosmic timeline: the curse is operational now, and the final justice will be executed then.\n\nFor the believer, this knowledge is both a warning and a source of strength. It is a warning not to follow the accursed one, and it is a source of strength to know that the enemy who whispers in their heart is already under Allah’s curse, his ultimate defeat is certain, and his power is limited to suggestion, not compulsion. The believer fights a cursed enemy, and with Allah’s help, can overcome him.",
  "79": "Upon hearing his condemnation and expulsion, Iblīs did not ask for forgiveness. Instead, he made a request that revealed his new, malevolent mission. “Satan appealed, ‘My Lord! Then delay my end until the Day of their resurrection.’” The address “My Lord” shows that even in his rebellion, Iblīs acknowledges Allah’s Lordship; he is not an atheist. He knows his Creator and yet chooses defiance. His request for a delay is not a plea for more time to repent, but for more time to exact revenge. He wants his death postponed so that he can live alongside human beings and dedicate himself to their misguidance.\n\nThe phrase “the Day of their resurrection” is specific: he wants to live until that great day when all humans are raised from their graves. He knows that after that day, his own punishment will commence fully, but he wants the entire span of human earthly life to be his field of operation. This request shows the depth of his malice. Having been dishonoured because of Adam, he does not direct his complaint solely at Allah but channels his rage towards Adam’s descendants. He wants to prove, in his own twisted way, that the clay creation is unworthy of the honour, by dragging as many of them as possible into his own state of curse. The appeal is granted in part, as the next verse shows, but it is a granting that serves as a test for humanity. The very existence of Satan as a tempter is part of the divine wisdom that allows moral choice to have real weight. Without an enemy whispering towards evil, the choice for good is less manifestly a choice of faith and love for Allah.",
  "80": "The request is answered, but with a precise qualification. “Allah said, ‘You will be delayed.’” The word “delayed” implies that Iblīs’s life is extended and his death is postponed, but not indefinitely or on his own terms. The respite is granted within limits.\n\nThe next verse will specify “until the appointed Day,” which clarifies that he will not live until the Day of Resurrection itself, but until a specific appointed time known to Allah. Most scholars explain that Iblīs will die after the first blowing of the Trumpet, which will cause all living beings on earth and in the heavens to die, thus ending his period of testing humanity. He will then be resurrected along with everyone else to face judgment. So the delay is real, granting him the time he asked for to tempt humanity, but it does not extend to the point where he escapes the very Day of Reckoning he wished to postpone. This limited respite is a manifestation of Allah’s wisdom and a mercy to the believers. It allows the drama of human life to unfold with its full moral dimensions. Every human being will face the whispers of Iblīs, but they will also have the guidance of revelation, the innate disposition towards God, and the assistance of the angels and righteous believers. The delayed enemy becomes the means by which true faith is distinguished from hypocrisy. The verse is a quiet but powerful reassurance: Satan’s time is limited, his power is circumscribed, and the very respite he demanded will ultimately lead to his own multiplied punishment as he bears the burdens of all whom he misled. The believer can therefore face the struggle against satanic whispers with patience and confidence, knowing that the enemy’s days are numbered and his defeat is certain.",
  "81": "Allah’s reply to Iblīs concludes with a precise time boundary: “until the appointed Day.” This clarifies that the delay granted to Satan is not an open-ended reprieve but a limited term fixed in Allah’s knowledge. The “appointed Day” refers to the moment decreed for the end of Iblīs’s respite. Most scholars of tafsir explain that this is not the Day of Resurrection itself but the time of the first blowing of the Trumpet, when all living beings on earth and in the heavens will swoon and die. Iblīs had asked to be delayed until the Day of their resurrection, but Allah granted him only until that earlier appointed moment, after which he too will taste death and be raised for judgment. This limit is profoundly significant. The entire period of human history, from the descent of Adam to the earth until the blowing of the Trumpet, is the arena of the satanic test, but it is bounded. Iblīs does not have unlimited time to carry out his schemes; there is a fixed terminus known only to Allah. This provides immense comfort to the believer. The enemy, despite his apparent freedom, is on a divine leash. His time is running out with every passing moment.\n\nThe phrase “appointed Day” also links back to the surah’s central theme of the Day of Reckoning. The entire cosmos is moving inexorably towards that appointed moment. Just as the earlier verses warned the disbelievers that they are awaiting nothing but a single Blast, so Iblīs is awaiting his appointed end. The delay granted to him is not an act of divine approval but a calculated period that serves a higher purpose: to test humanity and to distinguish the sincere from the hypocrite. The appointed Day will arrive without delay, and on that day, all the arrogant plans of both human and jinn devils will crumble. The verse subtly reminds every listener that their own appointed term of life is also fixed, and the temptation to delay repentance or to follow satanic whispers must be resisted while the door remains open.",
  "82": "Upon receiving the respite, Iblīs does not express gratitude or show any sign of relenting. Instead, he swears a mighty oath to intensify his war against humanity. “Satan said, ‘By Your Glory! I will certainly mislead them all.’” The oath is staggering in its audacity. He swears by the Glory of Allah, the very attribute of divine majesty and perfection that he is defying. This shows that even in his rebellion, Iblīs cannot deny Allah’s absolute glory; he exploits the sacredness of an oath by Allah to give weight to his evil vow. Swearing by Allah’s Glory is the most serious oath one can take, and Iblīs uses it to pledge his complete dedication to misguidance.\n\nThe word “certainly” (la) adds an emphasis of determination. His declared mission is to “mislead them all,” meaning to divert every descendant of Adam from the straight path. He does not aim to mislead only a few; his goal is total destruction.\n\nThe phrase “mislead them” translates aghwiyannahum, which implies causing them to fall into error, to stray from the right course, and to be ruined. The method of misleading is described elsewhere in the Quran: he will come to them from before, behind, right, and left, using every avenue of temptation.\n\nHowever, it is crucial to understand the limits of his power. He can only whisper, beautify falsehood, and invite to evil; he cannot compel anyone. The Quran explicitly says, “There is no authority for him over those who believe and rely upon their Lord.” His oath is a declaration of intent, but it does not grant him control over hearts that sincerely seek refuge in Allah. The verse paints a picture of an enemy utterly consumed by malice, whose very being has become defined by his mission to drag others into his own ruin. The believer is warned never to underestimate this enemy, yet also reminded that his power is no match for sincere faith.",
  "83": "Even as Iblīs swears his universal campaign of misguidance, he is forced to acknowledge a group that will remain beyond his reach. “Except Your chosen servants among them.\n\n” The word “chosen” translates mukhlaṣīn, which can be read as “those who are purely devoted to You” (mukhliṣīn) or “those whom You have purified and selected” (mukhlaṣīn). Both meanings are intertwined and profound. The chosen servants are those whom Allah has safeguarded, purified from shirk and major sins, and granted sincerity of worship. They are the ones who truly embody the declaration that there is no god but Allah. Iblīs admits that he cannot overcome them. He knows that his whispers cannot penetrate a heart filled with sincere devotion and constant reliance on Allah. This admission is a backhanded testimony to the power of ikhlāṣ, sincerity. The very enemy of humanity confesses that he is helpless against those who belong completely to Allah. In another surah, Iblīs says, “I will sit in ambush for them on Your straight path… then I will come to them from before them, from behind them, from their right and from their left, and You will find most of them ungrateful.” Here, however, he excepts the chosen ones, acknowledging that his ambush will fail against them. This teaches that the fortress against Satan is not intellectual brilliance or worldly power, but a heart purified for Allah alone. The chosen servants are described elsewhere as those who remember Allah often, who pray with humility, who spend from their wealth, and who fear the Day of Judgment. They are the muttaqūn, the righteous, whose honourable destination was described earlier in the surah. Iblīs’s exception is not a concession of mercy on his part; it is a statement of fact forced out of him by the reality of Allah’s protection.\n\nFor the believer, this verse is an invitation to strive to be among those chosen, protected servants by purifying their faith and turning wholly to Allah.",
  "84": "The narrative now reaches its climax with the final, decisive word from the Lord of all worlds. “Allah concluded, ‘The truth is—and I only say the truth—:’.\n\n” The Arabic word qāla (He said) indicates a definitive pronouncement. What follows is not a negotiation or a conditional statement; it is the unalterable divine decree that settles the matter once and for all. Before delivering the content of the decree, Allah inserts a powerful parenthetical statement: “and I only say the truth.” This serves as an absolute affirmation of the veracity and certainty of what is about to be declared. Allah does not need to take an oath by His glory as Iblīs did; He simply states that His word is truth itself. This phrase establishes that everything that follows is irrevocable, bound by divine truthfulness, and will certainly come to pass. It also serves as a contrast to Iblīs’s earlier oath. Iblīs swore by Allah’s glory to mislead, but his oath, however emphatic, is the word of a creature. Allah’s word is the ultimate reality that cannot be altered or prevented.\n\nThe repetition of “truth” (ḥaqq) anchors the entire conflict in the divine perspective. The cosmic drama of Iblīs’s rebellion, the test of humanity, and the final judgment all flow from the truth and return to it.\n\nThis verse also reassures the Prophet and the believers that despite the apparent strength of falsehood and the success of satanic whispers in misleading many, the ultimate outcome is determined by the truth of Allah’s decree. The truth will prevail, and falsehood will vanish. The stage is now set for the pronouncement of the final judgment, which leaves no ambiguity about the fate of the arrogant and the misguided.",
  "85": "The divine decree is delivered with terrifying clarity and certainty. “I will surely fill up Hell with you and whoever follows you from among them, all together.\n\n” The word “surely” (la-amlaʾanna) is an emphatic oath, indicating that this is a sworn, unbreakable decree. Hell is depicted as a receptacle that will be filled to capacity.\n\nThe first occupants are Iblīs himself and his direct progeny among the jinn. Then the decree extends to “whoever follows you from among them,” meaning those human beings who choose to follow Satan’s path of rebellion, arrogance, and rejection of the truth.\n\nThe phrase “all together” (ajmaʿīn) emphasizes that none of the followers will escape; they will be gathered in Hell en masse, just as the angels prostrated all together to Adam. This is the symmetrical justice of the divine decree: those who follow the one who refused to prostrate will share his punishment. The verse is a direct fulfilment of the warning given throughout the surah to the disbelieving Quraysh and to all who tread the path of arrogance. It also answers the earlier rhetorical question: “Or should We treat those who believe and do good like those who make mischief throughout the land?\n\n” The answer is a resounding no. The followers of Satan will fill Hell, and the righteous will fill Paradise. The imagery of “filling” Hell implies that it is vast and that many will enter it. This should cause every heart to tremble and to examine its allegiances. Whose path am I following? The path of the prophets and the righteous, or the path of Iblīs and his followers? The verse does not leave room for neutrality; one either follows divine guidance or is at risk of being swept into the satanic camp.\n\nYet, it also contains a hidden mercy: the decree is against those who follow Iblīs. The door of repentance remains open for anyone who abandons that following and turns back to Allah. The filling of Hell is a consequence of human choice, not an arbitrary fate, and Allah’s truthfulness ensures that both the promise of Paradise and the threat of Hell are real and certain.",
  "86": "With the narrative of Iblīs complete, the surah returns to the Prophet’s mission, addressing the specific accusations of the Quraysh. “Say, O Prophet, ‘I do not ask you for any reward for this Quran.’” The Prophet is instructed to publicly declare that he seeks no material compensation, no wealth, no status, and no power in return for delivering the message. This distinguishes him from the false claimants and the worldly leaders. He is not an opportunist exploiting religion for personal gain. His life of poverty, simplicity, and hardship was the living proof of this claim. The disbelievers had accused him of seeking power, but his actions consistently demonstrated the opposite.\n\nThe phrase “for this Quran” emphasizes that the revelation itself is a priceless gift that cannot be exchanged for any worldly consideration. Then comes the second part of the disavowal: “nor do I pretend to be someone I am not. ” The Arabic wa mā ana min al-mutakallifīn means he is not one of those who put on airs, who fabricate claims, or who artificially assume a role that does not belong to them. He does not pretend to be an angel, a lord, or a possessor of the treasures of Allah. He does not claim knowledge of the unseen except what is revealed. He is exactly what he declares himself to be: a human messenger, a warner, a servant of Allah. This verse is a powerful lesson in the purity of prophetic character. The messenger of truth has nothing to hide and no hidden agenda. His message is free, and his identity is transparent. This transparency stands in stark opposition to the devious nature of Iblīs, who disguised his arrogance behind a veneer of worship. The believer is called to emulate this sincerity and simplicity, avoiding the burden of pretension and the exploitation of religion for worldly ends.",
  "87": "The Prophet’s disavowal of reward and pretension is followed by a concise, majestic statement of the Quran’s universal purpose. “It is only a reminder to the whole world.\n\n” The word “reminder” (dhikr) links back to the very opening of the surah, where Allah swore by the Quran full of reminders. The entire Book, with its stories of past prophets, its descriptions of Paradise and Hell, and its account of Iblīs, is a comprehensive reminder of what every soul needs to know: its origin, its purpose, its enemy, its duties, and its final return.\n\nThe phrase “to the whole world” translates lil-ʿālamīn, meaning all of humankind and jinn, across all times and places. The message is not confined to the Arabs, or to a particular era, or to a specific class of people. It is universal and eternal. This universal scope answers the Quraysh’s narrow, tribal thinking, where they wondered why the revelation was sent to one of them rather than to a great man from their two cities. The Quran is a reminder to the whole world, and the Prophet’s being from the Arabs is an honour for them, not a reason for rejection.\n\nThe word “only” (in huwa illā) restricts the essential nature of the Quran to being a reminder. It is not a book of science, though it contains verses that inspire scientific reflection; it is not a history book, though it contains true history; it is fundamentally a dhikr, a remembrance, an admonition, a wake-up call for the sleeping hearts. The response required from the world is to heed the reminder, to remember what is already inscribed in the primordial human nature, and to align one’s life with that truth. The verse elevates the status of the Quran and simultaneously humbles the Prophet, for he is merely the conveyor of this universal reminder. It is a gift of mercy from the Lord of all worlds to His creation, and the only sensible response is gratitude, acceptance, and a life of remembrance.",
  "88": "The surah concludes with a powerful, ominous promise that serves as both a warning and a prediction. “And you will certainly know its truth before long.” The “you” here is addressed to the disbelievers who reject the Quran and mock the Prophet. The “its truth” refers to the truthfulness of the Quran’s message, its warnings, and its descriptions of the afterlife.\n\nThe phrase “before long” translates baʿda ḥīn, meaning after a period of time, not in the distant future but soon. This prophecy was fulfilled in multiple ways. In the short term, many of the Quraysh witnessed the truth of the Quran when they saw the defeat at Badr and other events that proved the Prophet’s words true. Some of them later embraced Islam and acknowledged that they had been wrong. In the longer term, the truth of the Quran became manifest through the spread of Islam, the conquest of Makkah, and the ultimate triumph of the message. And beyond all worldly events, the full truth will be known on the Day of Resurrection, when every soul will stand before Allah and see with absolute clarity what it had denied. The verse is not merely a threat but an invitation. It tells the disbelievers that their ignorance is temporary, and they have an opportunity to recognise the truth now, while faith can still benefit them. If they wait until the appointed time, the knowing will come, but it will be too late. The surah thus ends as it began, with an emphasis on the Quran as a reminder. It opened with the oath by the Quran full of reminders and the accusation that the disbelievers are in arrogance and opposition. It closes with the assurance that they will come to know the truth of that very reminder. The arc is complete: the message has been delivered, the stories of the prophets have been recounted, the fate of the righteous and the wicked has been detailed, the origin of satanic enmity has been exposed, and the Prophet has been vindicated. What remains is for each soul to choose its path before the time of knowing arrives and the window of repentance closes."
};
